PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L05U3.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":5,"cefr":"B1a","unit":3,"theme":"The World of Science","essential_question":"How do scientists find answers to questions?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["experiment","observe","hypothesis","evidence","result","measure","record","laboratory","microscope","predict","conclusion","data","variable","investigate"],"recycled_words":["water","sand","filter","first","next","finally","because","so","as a result","timeline","steps","biography","safe"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS1","title":"Word Power: The World of Science","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 science words and 4 Tier 2 words (procedure, accurate, analyze, identify)","use Greek roots (micro, scope, graph, tele, photo, bio, therm, meter) to work out the meanings of science words","build and explain new words from two roots (micro + scope = microscope)"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة علمية و4 كلمات أكاديمية.","استخدام الجذور اليونانية لفهم معاني الكلمات العلمية.","بناء كلمات جديدة من جذرين وشرحها."],"success_criteria":["I can use the science words correctly in sentences.","I can say what each Greek root means.","I can work out a new word from its roots and check it in context."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام الكلمات العلمية في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع قول معنى كل جذر يوناني.","أستطيع فهم كلمة جديدة من جذورها والتحقق منها في السياق."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"experiment","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪkˈspɛrəmənt/","definition":"a careful test to find out something or to check an idea","arabic":"تجربة","example":"We did an experiment with water and sand.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"observe","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əbˈzɝv/","definition":"to watch carefully to learn something","arabic":"يلاحظ","example":"Observe the color of the water.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"hypothesis","pos":"noun","ipa":"/haɪˈpɑθəsɪs/","definition":"a smart guess that you can test (plural: hypotheses)","arabic":"فرضية","example":"Our hypothesis was that more sand would make cleaner water.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"evidence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɛvədəns/","definition":"facts or information that show something is true","arabic":"دليل","example":"The clear water was evidence that the filter worked.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"result","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈzʌlt/","definition":"what happens at the end of an experiment","arabic":"نتيجة","example":"The result surprised us.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"measure","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈmɛʒər/","definition":"to find the size, amount or speed of something","arabic":"يقيس","example":"Measure 200 milliliters of water.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"record","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈkɔrd/","definition":"to write down information so you can use it later","arabic":"يسجّل","example":"Record your results in a table.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"laboratory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlæbrəˌtɔri/","definition":"a room where scientists do experiments (short: lab)","arabic":"مختبر","example":"We wore safety goggles in the laboratory.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"microscope","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmaɪkrəˌskoʊp/","definition":"a tool that makes very small things look much bigger","arabic":"مجهر","example":"We looked at a leaf under a microscope.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"predict","pos":"verb","ipa":"/prɪˈdɪkt/","definition":"to say what you think will happen","arabic":"يتنبأ","example":"Predict what will happen to the muddy water.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"conclusion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈkluʒən/","definition":"what you decide after looking at the results","arabic":"استنتاج / خلاصة","example":"Our conclusion was that sand removes dirt.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"data","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdeɪtə/","definition":"facts and numbers collected to study something","arabic":"بيانات","example":"We collected data about the water's color.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"variable","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɛriəbəl/","definition":"a thing that can change in an experiment","arabic":"متغيّر","example":"The number of sand layers was our variable.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"investigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪnˈvɛstəˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to look carefully at something to find the truth","arabic":"يحقق / يستقصي","example":"Scientists investigate how water can be cleaned.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"procedure","pos":"noun","ipa":"/prəˈsidʒər/","definition":"a set of steps for doing something in the correct way","arabic":"إجراء / خطوات العمل","example":"Follow the procedure step by step.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"accurate","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈækjərət/","definition":"exactly correct, with no mistakes","arabic":"دقيق","example":"Use a ruler to get accurate measurements.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"analyze","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈænəˌlaɪz/","definition":"to study something carefully to understand it","arabic":"يحلل","example":"Analyze the data to find a pattern.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"identify","pos":"verb","ipa":"/aɪˈdɛntəˌfaɪ/","definition":"to recognize and name something","arabic":"يحدد / يتعرّف على","example":"Identify the variable in the experiment.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"Many science words in English come from **Greek roots** – small word parts with their own meanings. If you know the roots, you can work out the meaning of long, difficult words.\n\nKey roots: **micro** = small · **scope** = look at, see · **graph** = write, draw, record · **tele** = far · **photo** = light · **bio** = life · **therm** = heat · **meter** = measure.\n\nSteps: (1) **Split** the word into roots: *micro + scope*. (2) **Translate** each root: small + look at. (3) **Put the meanings together**: a tool to look at small things. (4) **Check** the meaning in the sentence or a dictionary.","examples":["**micro + scope** = *microscope* → a tool for looking at very small things.","**tele + scope** = *telescope* → a tool for looking at things far away (stars, planets).","**photo + graph** = *photograph* → a picture 'written' with light.","**bio + graphy** = *biography* → writing about someone's life.","**therm + o + meter** = *thermometer* → a tool that measures heat (temperature).","**auto + graph** = *autograph* → writing your own name (auto = self).","**biology** = *bio* (life) + *logy* (study of) → the study of living things.","**micro + organism** = *microorganism* → a living thing too small to see without a microscope.","In context: *The scientist used a **telescope** to observe the moon.* → far + look = a tool to see far away. ✔"],"common_errors":["✗ A telescope makes small things bigger. → ✓ A telescope helps you see things that are far away. (tele = far; micro = small.)","✗ A biography is about animals. → ✓ A biography is about a person's life. (bio = life (of a person here).)","✗ Guessing from one root only. → ✓ Use ALL the roots in the word. (photo + graph together.)","✗ The datas show… → ✓ The data show… / The data shows… (data has no -s.)","✗ We did a hypothesis. → ✓ We made / tested a hypothesis. (make / test a hypothesis.)","✗ Record the results in your notebook it. → ✓ Record the results in your notebook. (No extra **it**.)","✗ The experiment was accurately. → ✓ The measurements were accurate. (be + adjective **accurate**.)"],"arabic":"كثير من الكلمات العلمية الإنجليزية مأخوذة من جذور يونانية لها معانٍ خاصة؛ فإذا عرفت الجذور فهمت الكلمات الطويلة.\n\nmicro = صغير، scope = ينظر، graph = يكتب/يرسم، tele = بعيد، photo = ضوء، bio = حياة، therm = حرارة، meter = قياس.\n\nالخطوات: قسّم الكلمة إلى جذور، وترجم كل جذر، واجمع المعاني، ثم تحقق من المعنى في الجملة أو المعجم.\n\nمثال: microscope = صغير + ينظر = أداة للنظر إلى الأشياء الصغيرة (مجهر)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Put on your safety goggles! Welcome to the world of science with Adam the Researcher!","on_screen":"The World of Science 🔬","visual":"Adam in his blue wheelchair and a lab coat in a school laboratory."},{"time":"00:25–01:20","narration":"Scientists ask questions, predict, make a hypothesis, do an experiment, observe, measure and record data!","on_screen":"predict · hypothesis · experiment · observe · measure · record · data","visual":"A quick animated science cycle."},{"time":"01:20–01:55","narration":"Then they analyze the results, identify the variable and write a conclusion based on evidence.","on_screen":"result · evidence · conclusion · variable · analyze · identify · accurate · procedure","visual":"A bar chart appears and a conclusion is written."},{"time":"01:55–03:05","narration":"Word detectives! micro = small, scope = look, tele = far, photo = light, graph = write, bio = life, therm = heat, meter = measure!","on_screen":"Greek roots 🏛️","visual":"Root blocks snap together: micro + scope → microscope."},{"time":"03:05–03:30","narration":"Split, translate, combine, check – and you can read any science word!","on_screen":"Split → Translate → Combine → Check","visual":"Adam looks through a microscope and smiles."}],"captions_ar":["ارتدِ نظارات السلامة! مرحبًا بكم في عالم العلوم مع آدم الباحث!","العلماء يسألون ويتنبؤون ويضعون فرضية ويجرون تجربة ويلاحظون ويقيسون ويسجلون البيانات!","ثم يحللون النتائج ويحددون المتغيّر ويكتبون استنتاجًا مبنيًا على الدليل.","محققو الكلمات! micro صغير، scope ينظر، tele بعيد، photo ضوء، graph يكتب، bio حياة، therm حرارة، meter قياس!","قسّم وترجم واجمع وتحقق، وستقرأ أي كلمة علمية!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, curious male science presenter, speed 0.95. Adam (the Researcher) appears in a lab coat."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","instructions_ar":"اختر الكلمة الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"A smart guess that you can test is a ___.","answer":"hypothesis","explanation":"Hypothesis.","explanation_ar":"فرضية.","options":["conclusion","hypothesis","laboratory","variable"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Facts and numbers collected in an experiment are ___.","answer":"data","explanation":"Data.","explanation_ar":"بيانات.","options":["procedure","microscope","data","hypothesis"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A room where scientists do experiments is a ___.","answer":"laboratory","explanation":"Laboratory.","explanation_ar":"مختبر.","options":["variable","conclusion","result","laboratory"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Use a measuring cup to ___ exactly 200 milliliters of water.","answer":"measure","explanation":"Measure.","explanation_ar":"تقيس.","options":["predict","measure","identify","investigate"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"After studying the data, the class wrote a ___: sand removes dirt from water.","answer":"conclusion","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"استنتاج.","options":["variable","procedure","conclusion","hypothesis"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"The thing we changed in the experiment – the number of sand layers – was the ___.","answer":"variable","explanation":"Variable.","explanation_ar":"المتغيّر.","options":["variable","evidence","laboratory","record"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Greek roots. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الجذور اليونانية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"The root *micro* means ___.","answer":"small","explanation":"micro.","explanation_ar":"صغير.","options":["small","far","life","heat"],"dok":1,"skill":"Greek roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"The root *scope* means ___.","answer":"look at, see","explanation":"scope.","explanation_ar":"ينظر.","options":["write","measure","light","look at, see"],"dok":1,"skill":"Greek roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"A *telescope* is a tool for looking at things that are ___.","answer":"far away","explanation":"tele = far.","explanation_ar":"بعيدة.","options":["very small","very hot","alive","far away"],"dok":2,"skill":"Greek roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What does a *thermometer* measure?","answer":"heat (temperature)","explanation":"therm + meter.","explanation_ar":"الحرارة.","options":["heat (temperature)","light","life","distance"],"dok":2,"skill":"Greek roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which word means *writing about a person's life*?","answer":"biography","explanation":"bio + graph.","explanation_ar":"سيرة.","options":["photograph","telescope","biography","thermometer"],"dok":2,"skill":"Greek roots","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*A **microorganism** is too small to see without help.* Using the roots, what is a microorganism?","answer":"a very small living thing","explanation":"micro + organism.","explanation_ar":"كائن حي دقيق.","options":["a big machine","a very small living thing","a tool for writing","a far planet"],"dok":3,"skill":"Greek roots","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Follow the ________ step by step so the experiment is fair.","answer":"procedure","explanation":"Steps.","explanation_ar":"خطوات العمل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Use a ruler to get ________ measurements.","answer":"accurate","explanation":"Exact.","explanation_ar":"دقيقة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"After the experiment, ________ the data to find a pattern.","answer":"analyze","explanation":"Study carefully.","explanation_ar":"حلّل.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Can you ________ the variable in this experiment?","answer":"identify","explanation":"Name it.","explanation_ar":"تحدد.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Before we start, ________ what will happen to the muddy water.","answer":"predict","explanation":"Say what you think.","explanation_ar":"تنبأ.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Scientists ________ problems by asking questions and testing ideas.","answer":"investigate","explanation":"Look into.","explanation_ar":"يستقصون.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words and roots.","instructions_ar":"اكتب بالكلمات الجديدة والجذور.","items":[{"q":"Split *photograph* into roots and explain its meaning.","answer":"photo (light) + graph (write) = a picture made with light.","explanation":"Roots.","explanation_ar":"الجذور.","dok":2,"skill":"Greek roots","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a hypothesis using *If … then …* about plants and sunlight.","answer":"If a plant gets more sunlight, then it will grow taller.","explanation":"Hypothesis.","explanation_ar":"فرضية.","dok":2,"skill":"science language","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain the difference between a *hypothesis* and a *conclusion*.","answer":"A hypothesis is a smart guess before the experiment; a conclusion is what you decide after you analyze the results.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"science language","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which science tool would you most like to use? Why? Use a root in your answer.","answer":"A telescope, because tele means far, and I want to look at the moon and the planets.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"ربط.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Root Lab","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Word lab: the child drags two root test tubes into a beaker; a real word bubbles up with its picture and meaning. Mixing roots that don't make a real word makes the beaker fizz and reset.","Each team picks two root cards from the beaker.","If they make a real word, they say its meaning from the roots.","They check the word in a dictionary.","Bonus: use the word in a science sentence.","Real word = 1 point; correct meaning = 1 point; good sentence = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is an *experiment*?","answer":"a careful test to find out something","explanation":"Experiment.","explanation_ar":"تجربة.","options":["a kind of microscope","a science book","a careful test to find out something","a final answer"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which root means *write* or *draw*?","answer":"graph","explanation":"graph.","explanation_ar":"graph.","options":["scope","micro","therm","graph"],"dok":1,"skill":"Greek roots"},{"q":"*The clear water was **evidence** that the filter worked.* *Evidence* means ___.","answer":"information that shows something is true","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"دليل.","options":["a guess","a tool","a mistake","information that shows something is true"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which tool makes very small things look bigger?","answer":"a microscope","explanation":"micro + scope.","explanation_ar":"المجهر.","options":["a microscope","a telescope","a thermometer","a photograph"],"dok":1,"skill":"Greek roots"},{"q":"Which sentence about the data is correct?","answer":"We recorded the data in a table.","explanation":"data (no -s).","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We recorded the datas in a table.","We recorded the data in a table.","We record the data in a table yesterday.","We recording the data in a table."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *hypothesis* correctly?","answer":"Our hypothesis was that more sand would make the water clearer.","explanation":"Usage.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We did a hypothesis at lunch.","Our hypothesis was that more sand would make the water clearer.","The hypothesis is the final answer.","Hypothesis the water."],"dok":2,"skill":"science language"},{"q":"What does *meter* mean in *thermometer*?","answer":"measure","explanation":"meter.","explanation_ar":"قياس.","options":["measure","light","far","life"],"dok":2,"skill":"Greek roots"},{"q":"Which order shows how scientists usually work?","answer":"question → hypothesis → experiment → results → conclusion","explanation":"Scientific method.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الصحيح.","options":["conclusion → question → results","results → hypothesis → question","question → hypothesis → experiment → results → conclusion","experiment → conclusion → question"],"dok":2,"skill":"science process"},{"q":"*The doctor looked at the **photomicrograph** of the cell.* Using the roots, what is it?","answer":"a picture taken through a microscope","explanation":"photo + micro + graph.","explanation_ar":"صورة مأخوذة عبر المجهر.","options":["a far-away star","a heat machine","a life story","a picture taken through a microscope"],"dok":3,"skill":"Greek roots"},{"q":"Why is knowing Greek roots useful in science lessons?","answer":"It helps you work out the meanings of long, new science words.","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"يساعد على فهم الكلمات العلمية.","options":["It makes words shorter.","It replaces experiments.","It helps you work out the meanings of long, new science words.","It helps you spell names of cities."],"dok":3,"skill":"strategy"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'experiment': students with beakers in a lab"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'observe': a student watching a beaker closely"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'hypothesis': a thought bubble with 'If… then…'"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'evidence': a magnifying glass over a clear jar"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'result': a chart with a star on one bar"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'measure': a measuring cup with water"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'record': a student writing in a notebook"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'laboratory': a school lab with benches and equipment"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'microscope': a student looking into a microscope"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'predict': a child pointing to a question mark"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'conclusion': a final page with a check mark"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'data': a table of numbers"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'variable': two jars with different amounts of sand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'investigate': a scientist with a notebook and a magnifying glass"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'procedure': a numbered list of steps"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'accurate': a ruler measuring exactly"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'analyze': a student studying a graph"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'identify': a finger pointing at one item in a group"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-01","prompt":"Students doing experiments in a school laboratory."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-02","prompt":"The steps of a science investigation."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS1-03","prompt":"Greek root blocks building science words."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V01","script":"experiment. We did an experiment with water and sand."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V02","script":"observe. Observe the color of the water."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V03","script":"hypothesis. Our hypothesis was that more sand would make cleaner water."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V04","script":"evidence. The clear water was evidence that the filter worked."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V05","script":"result. The result surprised us."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V06","script":"measure. Measure 200 milliliters of water."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V07","script":"record. Record your results in a table."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V08","script":"laboratory. We wore safety goggles in the laboratory."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V09","script":"microscope. We looked at a leaf under a microscope."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V10","script":"predict. Predict what will happen to the muddy water."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V11","script":"conclusion. Our conclusion was that sand removes dirt."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V12","script":"data. We collected data about the water's color."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V13","script":"variable. The number of sand layers was our variable."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V14","script":"investigate. Scientists investigate how water can be cleaned."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V15","script":"procedure. Follow the procedure step by step."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V16","script":"accurate. Use a ruler to get accurate measurements."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V17","script":"analyze. Analyze the data to find a pattern."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-V18","script":"identify. Identify the variable in the experiment."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS1-VID","script":"Put on your safety goggles! Welcome to the world of science with Adam the Researcher! Scientists ask questions, predict, make a hypothesis, do an experiment, observe, measure and record data! Then they analyze the results, identify the variable and write a conclusion based on evidence. Word detectives! micro = small, scope = look, tele = far, photo = light, graph = write, bio = life, therm = heat, meter = measure! Split, translate, combine, check – and you can read any science word!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS2","title":"Build a Simple Water Filter","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a science procedure of about 390 words: question, hypothesis, materials, safety, steps and explanation","explain the purpose of each step (why the scientist does it)","use evidence from the text to explain how the filter works and what it cannot do"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة إجراء علمي من نحو 400 كلمة: السؤال والفرضية والأدوات والسلامة والخطوات والشرح.","شرح الغرض من كل خطوة.","استخدام الدليل من النص لشرح كيف يعمل المرشح وما لا يستطيع فعله."],"success_criteria":["I can find the question, the hypothesis and the safety rules.","I can explain why a step is important.","I can explain why filtered water is still not safe to drink."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع إيجاد السؤال والفرضية وقواعد السلامة.","أستطيع شرح أهمية الخطوة.","أستطيع شرح لماذا لا يكون الماء المرشّح آمنًا للشرب."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"filter","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈfɪltər/","definition":"a thing that lets liquid pass but catches dirt; to clean something this way","arabic":"مرشّح / يرشّح","example":"The filter caught the dirt.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"funnel","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfʌnəl/","definition":"a tube that is wide at the top and narrow at the bottom","arabic":"قمع","example":"The top of the bottle works like a funnel.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"gravel","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɡrævəl/","definition":"small stones","arabic":"حصى","example":"Put the gravel on the top layer.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"coarse","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kɔrs/","definition":"made of large, rough pieces","arabic":"خشن (حبيبات كبيرة)","example":"Coarse sand has bigger grains than fine sand.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"rinse","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪns/","definition":"to wash something quickly with clean water","arabic":"يشطف","example":"Rinse the sand before you use it.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"particle","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpɑrtɪkəl/","definition":"a very small piece of something","arabic":"جُسيم","example":"Fine sand catches the tiniest particles.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"trap","pos":"verb","ipa":"/træp/","definition":"to catch and hold something so it cannot escape","arabic":"يحتجز","example":"The layers trap the dirt.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **science procedure** is written in a special order: a **question**, a **hypothesis** (*If … then … because …*), a list of **materials**, **safety** rules, numbered **steps**, what to **observe and record**, and an **explanation** of what is happening.\n\nGood readers don't just follow the steps – they ask **Why?** Every step has a **purpose**. For example, *Rinse the layers* → so that the first dirty water from the sand doesn't spoil the result.\n\nAlso read the **safety** notes and the **limits**: what the experiment can and **cannot** show. Here, the filter makes water **clearer**, but it does **not** make water **safe to drink**.","examples":["**Question:** *Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer?* – what we want to find out.","**Hypothesis:** *If muddy water passes through layers…, then it will come out clearer, because the layers will trap the dirt.*","**Step 1 – purpose:** the upside-down top works **like a funnel**, so water flows down through the layers.","**Step 3 – purpose:** the order **fine → coarse → gravel** (from the bottom up) means big dirt is caught first, then smaller dirt.","**Step 4 – purpose:** rinsing removes dust from the sand, so the test is **fair**.","**Step 5 – purpose:** measuring **200 milliliters** each time makes the test **accurate** and easy to compare.","**Explanation:** *The gravel catches the biggest pieces… the fine sand and the coffee filter catch the tiniest particles.*","**Limit:** *a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see.*","**Safety:** *Never drink the water from your filter.*"],"common_errors":["✗ Skipping the safety notes. → ✓ Read them first – they protect you. (Safety first.)","✗ Clear water is always safe to drink. → ✓ Clear water can still contain germs. (Check the facts!)","✗ Changing the order of the steps. → ✓ Follow the numbers; each step prepares the next. (Order matters.)","✗ Pour the water fastly. → ✓ Pour the water slowly. (Adverb: **slowly**.)","✗ Put the layers on this order. → ✓ Add the layers in this order. (**in** this order.)","✗ The sand trap the dirt. → ✓ The sand traps the dirt. (Singular subject → **traps**.)"],"arabic":"يُكتب الإجراء العلمي بترتيب خاص: سؤال، فرضية (إذا… فإن… لأن…)، أدوات، قواعد سلامة، خطوات مرقّمة، ما نلاحظه ونسجله، وشرح لما يحدث.\n\nالقارئ الجيد لا يتبع الخطوات فقط، بل يسأل: لماذا؟ لكل خطوة غرض، مثل شطف الطبقات حتى لا يفسد الماء المتسخ الأول النتيجة.\n\nاقرأ قواعد السلامة وحدود التجربة: المرشح يجعل الماء أصفى لكنه لا يجعله صالحًا للشرب.\n\nمعلومة مهمة: الماء الصافي قد يحتوي على جراثيم لا تُرى بالعين."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Muddy water in, clearer water out? Let's build a filter and find out!","on_screen":"Build a Simple Water Filter 💧","visual":"A jar of brown muddy water next to an empty bottle filter."},{"time":"00:25–02:00","narration":"Around the world, millions of people do not have clean water…","on_screen":"(procedure, step by step)","visual":"An adult cuts the bottle; Sami adds cotton, a coffee filter, fine sand, coarse sand and gravel; Adam measures 200 mL and starts a timer; water drips into a cup."},{"time":"02:00–02:40","narration":"Why each step? The funnel guides the water, rinsing keeps the test fair, measuring makes it accurate!","on_screen":"Step → Purpose","visual":"Arrows link each step to its purpose."},{"time":"02:40–03:15","narration":"Gravel catches big dirt, sand catches smaller dirt… but germs are too small! Never drink it.","on_screen":"⚠️ Clear ≠ safe","visual":"A zoom shows tiny germs still in the clear water."},{"time":"03:15–03:30","narration":"Change one variable and test again. That's how scientists learn!","on_screen":"Take it further 🔬","visual":"Two filters side by side: one with more sand."}],"captions_ar":["ماء موحل يدخل وماء أصفى يخرج؟ لنبنِ مرشحًا ونكتشف!","حول العالم لا يجد ملايين الناس ماءً نظيفًا قرب منازلهم…","لماذا كل خطوة؟ القمع يوجه الماء، والشطف يجعل الاختبار عادلًا، والقياس يجعله دقيقًا!","الحصى يحتجز الأوساخ الكبيرة والرمل يحتجز الأصغر… لكن الجراثيم صغيرة جدًا! لا تشربه أبدًا.","غيّر متغيرًا واحدًا وأعد الاختبار؛ هكذا يتعلم العلماء!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly female science teacher, speed 0.95. Sami and Adam do the experiment."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Build a Simple Water Filter","body":"Around the world, millions of people do not have clean water near their homes. Engineers design filters to remove dirt from water. In this investigation, you will build a simple filter and test how well it works.\n\n**Question:** Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer?\n\n**Hypothesis:** If muddy water passes through layers of sand and gravel, then it will come out clearer, because the layers will trap the dirt.\n\n**Materials:** a large clear plastic bottle, scissors, a cotton ball, a coffee filter, clean fine sand, clean coarse sand, small gravel, a clear cup, a measuring cup, a jar of muddy water and a timer.\n\n**Safety:** Ask an adult to cut the bottle. Wash your hands after the experiment. Never drink the water from your filter. It may look clean, but it can still contain germs.\n\n**Procedure**\n\n**1.** Ask an adult to cut the plastic bottle in half. Turn the top half upside down and place it in the bottom half like a funnel.\n\n**2.** Push the cotton ball into the neck of the bottle. Lay the coffee filter on top of it.\n\n**3.** Add the layers in this order: fine sand, then coarse sand, then gravel. Make each layer about three centimeters deep.\n\n**4.** Pour clean water through the filter once to rinse the layers. Throw this water away.\n\n**5.** Stir the muddy water. Measure 200 milliliters and pour it slowly into the top of the filter. Start the timer.\n\n**6.** When the water stops dripping, stop the timer. Pour the filtered water into a clear cup.\n\n**Observe and Record**\n\nCompare the filtered water with the muddy water. Record the color, how cloudy it looks, how much water came through and how long it took. Draw both cups in your notebook.\n\n**What Is Happening?**\n\nThe gravel catches the biggest pieces of dirt. The coarse sand traps smaller pieces, and the fine sand and the coffee filter catch the tiniest particles. Each layer cleans the water a little more. However, a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see. That is why real water plants also use special treatments to make water safe to drink.\n\n**Take It Further**\n\nChange one variable and test again. For example, use only gravel, or make the sand layer twice as deep. Predict what will happen, then compare your results.","word_count":392,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U3-LS2-1","genre":"procedural","role":"main","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[{"word":"funnel","definition":"a tube that is wide at the top and narrow at the bottom","arabic":"قمع"},{"word":"gravel","definition":"small stones","arabic":"حصى"},{"word":"particle","definition":"a very small piece of something","arabic":"جُسيم"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the procedure. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ الإجراء واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What QUESTION does the investigation try to answer?","answer":"Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer?","explanation":"Question.","explanation_ar":"السؤال.","options":["How do plants drink water?","Why is the sea salty?","Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer?","How fast does water boil?"],"dok":1,"skill":"text structure","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who should cut the plastic bottle?","answer":"an adult","explanation":"Safety.","explanation_ar":"شخص بالغ.","options":["the youngest student","an adult","nobody – use it whole","the class together"],"dok":1,"skill":"safety","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What goes into the neck of the bottle first?","answer":"a cotton ball","explanation":"Step 2.","explanation_ar":"قطنة.","options":["gravel","muddy water","fine sand","a cotton ball"],"dok":1,"skill":"sequence","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How deep should each layer be?","answer":"about three centimeters","explanation":"Step 3.","explanation_ar":"نحو 3 سم.","options":["about thirty centimeters","about three centimeters","one millimeter","as deep as the bottle"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How much muddy water do you pour into the filter?","answer":"200 milliliters","explanation":"Step 5.","explanation_ar":"200 مل.","options":["200 milliliters","2 liters","20 milliliters","a full bucket"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which layer catches the BIGGEST pieces of dirt?","answer":"the gravel","explanation":"What Is Happening?","explanation_ar":"الحصى.","options":["the gravel","the fine sand","the coffee filter","the cotton ball"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: The purpose of each step. Choose.","instructions_ar":"الغرض من كل خطوة: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Why do you turn the top half of the bottle upside down?","answer":"so it works like a funnel and guides the water through the layers","explanation":"Step 1 purpose.","explanation_ar":"ليعمل كقمع.","options":["so it looks nicer","so the water stays in the top","so the sand falls out","so it works like a funnel and guides the water through the layers"],"dok":2,"skill":"purpose of steps","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why do you pour clean water through the filter first and throw it away?","answer":"to rinse dust from the layers so the test is fair","explanation":"Step 4 purpose.","explanation_ar":"لشطف الطبقات.","options":["to make the sand wet for fun","to fill the cup","to rinse dust from the layers so the test is fair","to measure the time"],"dok":2,"skill":"purpose of steps","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why do you measure exactly 200 milliliters?","answer":"to make the test accurate and easy to compare with other tests","explanation":"Step 5 purpose.","explanation_ar":"ليكون الاختبار دقيقًا.","options":["to make the test accurate and easy to compare with other tests","because 200 is a lucky number","to make the water clearer","to make the filter faster"],"dok":2,"skill":"purpose of steps","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why should you NEVER drink the water from your filter?","answer":"It can still contain germs or chemicals that are too small to see.","explanation":"Safety + limit.","explanation_ar":"قد تبقى فيه جراثيم.","options":["It tastes like coffee.","It is too cold.","It is too clear.","It can still contain germs or chemicals that are too small to see."],"dok":2,"skill":"safety","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which part of the hypothesis gives the REASON?","answer":"because the layers will trap the dirt","explanation":"Hypothesis structure.","explanation_ar":"because…","options":["If muddy water passes through layers","then it will come out clearer","because the layers will trap the dirt","of sand and gravel"],"dok":2,"skill":"hypothesis","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In *Take It Further*, what does *change one variable* mean?","answer":"change only one thing, such as the depth of the sand, and keep everything else the same","explanation":"Fair test.","explanation_ar":"غيّر شيئًا واحدًا فقط.","options":["change everything at once","change only one thing, such as the depth of the sand, and keep everything else the same","stop the experiment","change the question"],"dok":3,"skill":"variables","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that explains what a simple filter CANNOT do.","answer":"However, a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see.","explanation":"Limit.","explanation_ar":"الحدود.","evidence":"However, a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U3-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["Around the world, millions of people do not have clean water near their homes.","Engineers design filters to remove dirt from water.","In this investigation, you will build a simple filter and test how well it works.","**Question:** Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer?","**Hypothesis:** If muddy water passes through layers of sand and gravel, then it will come out clearer, because the layers will trap the dirt.","**Materials:** a large clear plastic bottle, scissors, a cotton ball, a coffee filter, clean fine sand, clean coarse sand, small gravel, a clear cup, a measuring cup, a jar of muddy water and a timer.","**Safety:** Ask an adult to cut the bottle.","Wash your hands after the experiment.","Never drink the water from your filter.","It may look clean, but it can still contain germs.","**Procedure**","**1.** Ask an adult to cut the plastic bottle in half.","Turn the top half upside down and place it in the bottom half like a funnel.","**2.** Push the cotton ball into the neck of the bottle.","Lay the coffee filter on top of it.","**3.** Add the layers in this order: fine sand, then coarse sand, then gravel.","Make each layer about three centimeters deep.","**4.** Pour clean water through the filter once to rinse the layers.","Throw this water away.","**5.** Stir the muddy water.","Measure 200 milliliters and pour it slowly into the top of the filter.","Start the timer.","**6.** When the water stops dripping, stop the timer.","Pour the filtered water into a clear cup.","**Observe and Record**","Compare the filtered water with the muddy water.","Record the color, how cloudy it looks, how much water came through and how long it took.","Draw both cups in your notebook.","**What Is Happening?**","The gravel catches the biggest pieces of dirt.","The coarse sand traps smaller pieces, and the fine sand and the coffee filter catch the tiniest particles.","Each layer cleans the water a little more.","However, a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see.","That is why real water plants also use special treatments to make water safe to drink.","**Take It Further**","Change one variable and test again.","For example, use only gravel, or make the sand layer twice as deep.","Predict what will happen, then compare your results."]},{"q":"Underline the sentence that tells you what to record.","answer":"Record the color, how cloudy it looks, how much water came through and how long it took.","explanation":"Record.","explanation_ar":"التسجيل.","evidence":"Record the color, how cloudy it looks, how much water came through and how long it took.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U3-LS2-1","dok":1,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["Around the world, millions of people do not have clean water near their homes.","Engineers design filters to remove dirt from water.","In this investigation, you will build a simple filter and test how well it works.","**Question:** Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer?","**Hypothesis:** If muddy water passes through layers of sand and gravel, then it will come out clearer, because the layers will trap the dirt.","**Materials:** a large clear plastic bottle, scissors, a cotton ball, a coffee filter, clean fine sand, clean coarse sand, small gravel, a clear cup, a measuring cup, a jar of muddy water and a timer.","**Safety:** Ask an adult to cut the bottle.","Wash your hands after the experiment.","Never drink the water from your filter.","It may look clean, but it can still contain germs.","**Procedure**","**1.** Ask an adult to cut the plastic bottle in half.","Turn the top half upside down and place it in the bottom half like a funnel.","**2.** Push the cotton ball into the neck of the bottle.","Lay the coffee filter on top of it.","**3.** Add the layers in this order: fine sand, then coarse sand, then gravel.","Make each layer about three centimeters deep.","**4.** Pour clean water through the filter once to rinse the layers.","Throw this water away.","**5.** Stir the muddy water.","Measure 200 milliliters and pour it slowly into the top of the filter.","Start the timer.","**6.** When the water stops dripping, stop the timer.","Pour the filtered water into a clear cup.","**Observe and Record**","Compare the filtered water with the muddy water.","Record the color, how cloudy it looks, how much water came through and how long it took.","Draw both cups in your notebook.","**What Is Happening?**","The gravel catches the biggest pieces of dirt.","The coarse sand traps smaller pieces, and the fine sand and the coffee filter catch the tiniest particles.","Each layer cleans the water a little more.","However, a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see.","That is why real water plants also use special treatments to make water safe to drink.","**Take It Further**","Change one variable and test again.","For example, use only gravel, or make the sand layer twice as deep.","Predict what will happen, then compare your results."]},{"q":"Why does the procedure begin with a paragraph about millions of people without clean water?","answer":"to show why this science matters in the real world","explanation":"Author's purpose.","explanation_ar":"ليُظهر أهمية العلم في الواقع.","options":["to show why this science matters in the real world","to tell a story about a family","to list the materials","to give the safety rules"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose","stage":"freer"},{"q":"What do real water plants do that this filter cannot?","answer":"use special treatments to make water safe to drink","explanation":"What Is Happening?","explanation_ar":"معالجات خاصة.","options":["use only gravel","use special treatments to make water safe to drink","use coffee filters","use plastic bottles"],"dok":2,"skill":"key details","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain the procedure.","instructions_ar":"اشرح الإجراء.","items":[{"q":"Explain in your own words how the filter cleans the water.","answer":"The gravel catches big dirt, the coarse sand catches smaller dirt, and the fine sand and filter catch the tiniest particles, so the water gets clearer at each layer.","explanation":"Explain.","explanation_ar":"اشرح.","dok":2,"skill":"explaining a process","stage":"production"},{"q":"Choose one step and explain its purpose: *We do this so that…*","answer":"Step 4: we rinse the layers so that dust from the sand doesn't make the filtered water dirty.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"الغرض.","dok":2,"skill":"purpose of steps","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a new hypothesis for the *Take It Further* test.","answer":"If the sand layer is twice as deep, then the water will come out clearer, because there is more sand to trap the dirt.","explanation":"Hypothesis.","explanation_ar":"فرضية.","dok":3,"skill":"hypothesis","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why is it important for people around the world to have safe, clean water?","answer":"Because dirty water can carry germs that make people sick, and everyone needs water to live.","explanation":"Connect.","explanation_ar":"ربط.","dok":3,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Build a Simple Water Filter\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Build a Simple Water Filter”.","answer":"392 words","title":"Build a Simple Water Filter","body":"Around the world, millions of people do not have clean water near their homes. Engineers design filters to remove dirt from water. In this investigation, you will build a simple filter and test how well it works.\n\n**Question:** Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer?\n\n**Hypothesis:** If muddy water passes through layers of sand and gravel, then it will come out clearer, because the layers will trap the dirt.\n\n**Materials:** a large clear plastic bottle, scissors, a cotton ball, a coffee filter, clean fine sand, clean coarse sand, small gravel, a clear cup, a measuring cup, a jar of muddy water and a timer.\n\n**Safety:** Ask an adult to cut the bottle. Wash your hands after the experiment. Never drink the water from your filter. It may look clean, but it can still contain germs.\n\n**Procedure**\n\n**1.** Ask an adult to cut the plastic bottle in half. Turn the top half upside down and place it in the bottom half like a funnel.\n\n**2.** Push the cotton ball into the neck of the bottle. Lay the coffee filter on top of it.\n\n**3.** Add the layers in this order: fine sand, then coarse sand, then gravel. Make each layer about three centimeters deep.\n\n**4.** Pour clean water through the filter once to rinse the layers. Throw this water away.\n\n**5.** Stir the muddy water. Measure 200 milliliters and pour it slowly into the top of the filter. Start the timer.\n\n**6.** When the water stops dripping, stop the timer. Pour the filtered water into a clear cup.\n\n**Observe and Record**\n\nCompare the filtered water with the muddy water. Record the color, how cloudy it looks, how much water came through and how long it took. Draw both cups in your notebook.\n\n**What Is Happening?**\n\nThe gravel catches the biggest pieces of dirt. The coarse sand traps smaller pieces, and the fine sand and the coffee filter catch the tiniest particles. Each layer cleans the water a little more. However, a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see. That is why real water plants also use special treatments to make water safe to drink.\n\n**Take It Further**\n\nChange one variable and test again. For example, use only gravel, or make the sand layer twice as deep. Predict what will happen, then compare your results.","words":392,"limit":250}],"time_limit_sec":250}],"game":{"name":"Lab Step Shuffle","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Virtual lab: the child drags steps into order to build a filter; each step only works if it is in the right place. Then the child matches each step to its purpose. A safety alarm rings if the child tries to drink the water!","Teams get mixed step cards and purpose cards.","They put the steps in order on the lab bench.","They match each step to its purpose card.","They add the correct safety card at the top.","Correct order = 3 points; each correct purpose = 1 point; correct safety card = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *hypothesis*?","answer":"a smart guess that can be tested","explanation":"Hypothesis.","explanation_ar":"فرضية.","options":["a list of materials","a smart guess that can be tested","the last step","a safety rule"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Rinse the layers before you start.* *Rinse* means ___.","answer":"wash quickly with clean water","explanation":"Rinse.","explanation_ar":"اشطف.","options":["dry in the sun","throw away","wash quickly with clean water","cut in half"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which part of the procedure tells you what you need?","answer":"Materials","explanation":"Text structure.","explanation_ar":"الأدوات.","options":["Question","What Is Happening?","Take It Further","Materials"],"dok":1,"skill":"text structure"},{"q":"What are *particles*?","answer":"very small pieces of something","explanation":"Glossary.","explanation_ar":"جسيمات.","options":["large stones","very small pieces of something","kinds of bottles","steps in a procedure"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which instruction about pouring is written correctly?","answer":"Pour the muddy water slowly into the filter.","explanation":"Imperative + adverb.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Pour the muddy water slow into the filter.","Pour the muddy water slowly into the filter.","Pouring slowly the muddy water into the filter.","You pours the muddy water slowly into the filter."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"What is the order of the layers from the BOTTOM to the TOP?","answer":"fine sand, coarse sand, gravel","explanation":"Step 3.","explanation_ar":"رمل ناعم ثم خشن ثم حصى.","options":["gravel, coarse sand, fine sand","coarse sand, gravel, fine sand","fine sand, coarse sand, gravel","gravel, fine sand, coarse sand"],"dok":2,"skill":"sequence"},{"q":"Why is the hypothesis written with *If … then … because …*?","answer":"It shows what will change, what we expect and why.","explanation":"Hypothesis.","explanation_ar":"يبيّن التغيير والتوقع والسبب.","options":["It shows what will change, what we expect and why.","It makes the text longer.","It lists the materials.","It gives the safety rules."],"dok":2,"skill":"hypothesis"},{"q":"A student's filtered water is clear. What should he do?","answer":"observe and record it, but not drink it","explanation":"Safety.","explanation_ar":"يلاحظ ويسجل ولا يشرب.","options":["observe and record it, but not drink it","drink it because it is clean","give it to his friend to drink","pour it into a water bottle for lunch"],"dok":2,"skill":"safety"},{"q":"What can you infer from *Each layer cleans the water a little more*?","answer":"More layers probably make the water clearer.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"طبقات أكثر = ماء أصفى.","options":["One layer is enough.","Layers make the water dirtier.","Water does not need filters.","More layers probably make the water clearer."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Which test would be FAIR for comparing two filters?","answer":"Use the same amount of the same muddy water in both and change only the sand depth.","explanation":"Fair test.","explanation_ar":"اختبار عادل.","options":["Use different amounts of water in each.","Use muddy water in one and clean water in the other.","Use the same amount of the same muddy water in both and change only the sand depth.","Change the sand, the gravel and the water all at once."],"dok":3,"skill":"variables"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'filter': water dripping through a filter"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'funnel': a plastic funnel"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'gravel': a pile of small stones"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'coarse': coarse sand next to fine sand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'rinse': water pouring over sand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'particle': tiny dots under a magnifying glass"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'trap': dirt caught between grains of sand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-01","prompt":"A labelled diagram of a homemade water filter."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-02","prompt":"Muddy water before and clearer water after filtering."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS2-03","prompt":"A lab team with roles."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V01","script":"filter. The filter caught the dirt."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V02","script":"funnel. The top of the bottle works like a funnel."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V03","script":"gravel. Put the gravel on the top layer."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V04","script":"coarse. Coarse sand has bigger grains than fine sand."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V05","script":"rinse. Rinse the sand before you use it."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V06","script":"particle. Fine sand catches the tiniest particles."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-V07","script":"trap. The layers trap the dirt."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-T1","script":"Around the world, millions of people do not have clean water near their homes. Engineers design filters to remove dirt from water. In this investigation, you will build a simple filter and test how well it works. **Question:** Can layers of sand and gravel make muddy water clearer? **Hypothesis:** If muddy water passes through layers of sand and gravel, then it will come out clearer, because the layers will trap the dirt. **Materials:** a large clear plastic bottle, scissors, a cotton ball, a coffee filter, clean fine sand, clean coarse sand, small gravel, a clear cup, a measuring cup, a jar of muddy water and a timer. **Safety:** Ask an adult to cut the bottle. Wash your hands after the experiment. Never drink the water from your filter. It may look clean, but it can still contain germs. **Procedure** **1.** Ask an adult to cut the plastic bottle in half. Turn the top half upside down and place it in the bottom half like a funnel. **2.** Push the cotton ball into the neck of the bottle. Lay the coffee filter on top of it. **3.** Add the layers in this order: fine sand, then coarse sand, then gravel. Make each layer about three centimeters deep. **4.** Pour clean water through the filter once to rinse the layers. Throw this water away. **5.** Stir the muddy water. Measure 200 milliliters and pour it slowly into the top of the filter. Start the timer. **6.** When the water stops dripping, stop the timer. Pour the filtered water into a clear cup. **Observe and Record** Compare the filtered water with the muddy water. Record the color, how cloudy it looks, how much water came through and how long it took. Draw both cups in your notebook. **What Is Happening?** The gravel catches the biggest pieces of dirt. The coarse sand traps smaller pieces, and the fine sand and the coffee filter catch the tiniest particles. Each layer cleans the water a little more. However, a simple filter cannot remove germs or chemicals that are too small to see. That is why real water plants also use special treatments to make water safe to drink. **Take It Further** Change one variable and test again. For example, use only gravel, or make the sand layer twice as deep. Predict what will happen, then compare your results."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS2-VID","script":"Muddy water in, clearer water out? Let's build a filter and find out! Around the world, millions of people do not have clean water… Why each step? The funnel guides the water, rinsing keeps the test fair, measuring makes it accurate! Gravel catches big dirt, sand catches smaller dirt… but germs are too small! Never drink it. Change one variable and test again. That's how scientists learn!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS3","title":"Text features in science texts: \"Dr. Hayat Sindi: Science for Everyone\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["identify text features in science texts: headings, bold words, captions, timelines, key-word boxes, numbered steps and diagrams","explain how each feature helps the reader","compare the features of a biography of a scientist and a science procedure"],"objectives_ar":["تحديد سمات النصوص العلمية: العناوين والكلمات البارزة والتعليقات والخطوط الزمنية وصناديق الكلمات والخطوات المرقمة والرسوم.","شرح كيف تساعد كل سمة القارئ.","مقارنة سمات سيرة عالمة وإجراء علمي."],"success_criteria":["I can name the text features I see.","I can use a feature to find information quickly.","I can explain why a writer chose a feature for a type of text."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تسمية السمات التي أراها في النص.","أستطيع استخدام السمة لإيجاد المعلومة بسرعة.","أستطيع شرح سبب اختيار الكاتب لسمة معينة لنوع النص."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Language, Craft, and Structure","vocabulary":[{"word":"biotechnology","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌbaɪoʊtɛkˈnɑlədʒi/","definition":"using living things, such as cells, to make useful products","arabic":"التقنية الحيوية","example":"Dr. Sindi studied biotechnology.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"caption","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkæpʃən/","definition":"words under a picture that explain it","arabic":"تعليق على الصورة","example":"Read the caption under the photo.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"timeline","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtaɪmˌlaɪn/","definition":"a line that shows events in the order they happened","arabic":"خط زمني","example":"The timeline shows her life in order.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"ambassador","pos":"noun","ipa":"/æmˈbæsədər/","definition":"a person chosen to represent an idea or a country","arabic":"سفير / سفيرة","example":"She became a Goodwill Ambassador for science.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"inventor","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪnˈvɛntər/","definition":"a person who creates something new","arabic":"مخترع","example":"The institute helps young inventors.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"cell","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sɛl/","definition":"the smallest living part of a plant or an animal","arabic":"خلية","example":"We saw cells under the microscope.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"feature","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfitʃər/","definition":"a special part of something","arabic":"سمة / ميزة","example":"Headings are a feature of science texts.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"**Text features** are the special parts of a text that are not ordinary sentences. They help readers **find**, **understand** and **remember** information.\n\nCommon science text features: **headings** (show what each part is about), **bold words** (important terms), **key-word boxes / glossaries** (definitions), **captions** (explain pictures), **timelines** (events in order), **diagrams with labels** (show parts), **numbered steps** (procedures) and **safety boxes**.\n\nDifferent texts use different features. A **procedure** needs numbered steps, materials and safety notes. A **biography** of a scientist uses headings for life stages, a timeline and a caption for the photo. Ask: **Which feature helps me answer my question fastest?**","examples":["**Heading:** *A Brave Journey* → tells you this part is about her travel and studies.","**Bold word:** ***biotechnology*** → an important term; the key-word box explains it.","**Key-word box:** *biotechnology – using living things, such as cells, to make useful products.*","**Caption:** *Dr. Hayat Sindi believes that science should help people everywhere…* → explains the photo and the main idea.","**Timeline:** *Makkah → London → Cambridge → paper tests → 2012 → 2013* → shows the order of her life quickly.","**Procedure features:** *Question, Hypothesis, Materials, Safety*, numbered steps **1–6**.","**Diagram with labels:** the water-filter picture shows each layer and its name.","**Using features:** *When did she join the Shura Council?* → look at the **timeline** → 2013.","**Comparing:** the biography has a **timeline**; the procedure has **numbered steps** – both show **order**, but of different things."],"common_errors":["✗ Skipping captions and boxes. → ✓ Read them – they often hold key facts. (Features carry information.)","✗ Reading every word to find one date. → ✓ Scan the timeline or headings first. (Use features to search.)","✗ Thinking headings are just decoration. → ✓ Headings tell you what each section is about. (Use them to predict.)","✗ She study in London. → ✓ She studied in London. (Past tense in biographies.)","✗ She became one of the first woman on the council. → ✓ She became one of the first women on the council. (**one of the** + plural.)","✗ The tests doesn't need electricity. → ✓ The tests don't need electricity. (Plural → **don't**.)"],"arabic":"سمات النص هي الأجزاء الخاصة غير الجمل العادية، وتساعد القارئ على إيجاد المعلومات وفهمها وتذكرها.\n\nمن سمات النصوص العلمية: العناوين، والكلمات البارزة، وصناديق الكلمات، والتعليقات على الصور، والخطوط الزمنية، والرسوم الموضحة، والخطوات المرقمة، وصناديق السلامة.\n\nتختلف السمات باختلاف النص: الإجراء يحتاج خطوات وأدوات وسلامة، والسيرة تحتاج عناوين لمراحل الحياة وخطًا زمنيًا وتعليقًا على الصورة.\n\nالدكتورة حياة سندي عالمة سعودية من مكة المكرمة، شاركت في تطوير اختبارات ورقية زهيدة الثمن للتشخيص."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"A girl from Makkah who dreamed of science… and helped people far away from hospitals. Meet Dr. Hayat Sindi!","on_screen":"Science for Everyone 🧪","visual":"A girl reading a science book; the page turns into a lab."},{"time":"00:25–01:50","narration":"Hayat Sindi grew up in Makkah in a large family…","on_screen":"(biography)","visual":"Animated scenes: Makkah, London, Cambridge, a stamp-sized paper test changing color, a UNESCO stage, young inventors."},{"time":"01:50–02:40","narration":"Text features help you find information fast: headings, bold words, captions, timelines, key-word boxes!","on_screen":"Text features 🔎","visual":"Labels pop onto the biography page."},{"time":"02:40–03:15","narration":"The procedure uses different features: numbered steps, materials and safety boxes!","on_screen":"Biography vs. Procedure","visual":"The two texts side by side with their features highlighted."},{"time":"03:15–03:30","narration":"Which feature answers your question fastest? Scan it!","on_screen":"Scan → find → understand","visual":"A magnifying glass jumps to the timeline: 2013."}],"captions_ar":["فتاة من مكة حلمت بالعلم… وساعدت أناسًا بعيدين عن المستشفيات. تعرّف إلى الدكتورة حياة سندي!","نشأت حياة سندي في مكة المكرمة في أسرة كبيرة…","سمات النص تساعدك على إيجاد المعلومات بسرعة: العناوين والكلمات البارزة والتعليقات والخطوط الزمنية وصناديق الكلمات!","الإجراء يستخدم سمات مختلفة: خطوات مرقمة وأدوات وصناديق سلامة!","أي سمة تجيب عن سؤالك أسرع؟ امسحها بعينك!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, inspiring female voice, speed 0.95."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Dr. Hayat Sindi: Science for Everyone","body":"**Photo caption:** Dr. Hayat Sindi believes that science should help people everywhere.\n\n**A Dream in Makkah** – Hayat Sindi grew up in Makkah in a large family. As a girl, she read about great scientists such as Ibn al-Haytham and Marie Curie, and she dreamed of becoming a scientist too.\n\n**A Brave Journey** – At first, her family worried about her studying so far from home, but they supported her dream. She traveled to London, worked hard to learn English and then studied science at King's College London. Later, she earned a PhD in **biotechnology** at the University of Cambridge.\n\n**Science for Everyone** – Dr. Sindi wanted her science to help people who live far from doctors and laboratories. With a team of scientists in the United States, she helped start a project that makes tiny **paper tests**, about the size of a postage stamp. With just one drop of blood, the paper changes color to show whether a person may have a health problem. The tests are cheap and do not need electricity.\n\n**Inspiring Others** – In 2012, UNESCO named her a Goodwill Ambassador for science. In 2013, she became one of the first women on Saudi Arabia's Shura Council. She also started an institute that helps young inventors in the Arab world turn their ideas into real products.\n\n**Timeline:** grew up in Makkah → studied in London → PhD at Cambridge → paper tests → UNESCO Goodwill Ambassador (2012) → Shura Council (2013)\n\n**Key word:** *biotechnology* – using living things, such as cells, to make useful products.","word_count":249,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U3-LS3-1","genre":"biography","role":"paired","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[{"word":"biotechnology","definition":"using living things, such as cells, to make useful products","arabic":"التقنية الحيوية"},{"word":"postage stamp","definition":"a small sticker you put on a letter before you mail it","arabic":"طابع بريدي"},{"word":"ambassador","definition":"a person chosen to represent an idea or a country","arabic":"سفير / سفيرة"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the biography. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ السيرة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where did Hayat Sindi grow up?","answer":"in Makkah","explanation":"A Dream in Makkah.","explanation_ar":"في مكة.","options":["in London","in Makkah","in Cambridge","in Jeddah"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U3-LS3-1","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which scientists did she read about as a girl?","answer":"Ibn al-Haytham and Marie Curie","explanation":"A Dream in Makkah.","explanation_ar":"ابن الهيثم وماري كوري.","options":["Ibn al-Haytham and Marie Curie","Ibn Battuta and Al-Jazari","Bell and Edison","the Wright brothers"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did she study for her PhD at Cambridge?","answer":"biotechnology","explanation":"A Brave Journey.","explanation_ar":"التقنية الحيوية.","options":["history","art","music","biotechnology"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How big are the paper tests?","answer":"about the size of a postage stamp","explanation":"Science for Everyone.","explanation_ar":"بحجم طابع البريد.","options":["as big as a book","as big as a table","too small to see","about the size of a postage stamp"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What do the paper tests need to work?","answer":"just one drop of blood – no electricity","explanation":"Science for Everyone.","explanation_ar":"قطرة دم فقط.","options":["a big laboratory","a computer and electricity","just one drop of blood – no electricity","a microscope"],"dok":2,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did she become in 2013?","answer":"one of the first women on Saudi Arabia's Shura Council","explanation":"Timeline.","explanation_ar":"عضوة في مجلس الشورى.","options":["one of the first women on Saudi Arabia's Shura Council","a teacher in Makkah","a UNESCO student","a doctor in London"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Text features. Choose.","instructions_ar":"سمات النص: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which feature would help you find the YEAR she became a Goodwill Ambassador fastest?","answer":"the timeline","explanation":"Timeline.","explanation_ar":"الخط الزمني.","options":["the photo caption","the key-word box","the first heading","the timeline"],"dok":1,"skill":"text features","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What is the job of the key-word box?","answer":"to explain an important word","explanation":"Key-word box.","explanation_ar":"شرح كلمة مهمة.","options":["to show a picture","to list materials","to give safety rules","to explain an important word"],"dok":1,"skill":"text features","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why is *biotechnology* in bold?","answer":"It is an important term that is explained in the text.","explanation":"Bold words.","explanation_ar":"مصطلح مهم.","options":["It is the title.","It is a mistake.","It is an important term that is explained in the text.","It is a name of a city."],"dok":2,"skill":"text features","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which feature does the water-filter PROCEDURE have that the biography does not?","answer":"numbered steps and a safety section","explanation":"Compare features.","explanation_ar":"خطوات مرقمة وسلامة.","options":["a photo caption","numbered steps and a safety section","a timeline","a key-word box"],"dok":2,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which heading tells you the section is about her invention?","answer":"Science for Everyone","explanation":"Headings.","explanation_ar":"Science for Everyone.","options":["A Dream in Makkah","A Brave Journey","Science for Everyone","Inspiring Others"],"dok":2,"skill":"text features","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why does a biography often include a TIMELINE?","answer":"to show the events of a person's life in order at a glance","explanation":"Purpose of features.","explanation_ar":"ليعرض أحداث الحياة بالترتيب.","options":["to show the events of a person's life in order at a glance","to give the steps of an experiment","to define new words","to explain a photo"],"dok":3,"skill":"text features","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Text Features Chart.","instructions_ar":"أكمل جدول سمات النص.","items":[{"q":"List the features in the biography.","answer":"a photo caption, headings, a bold word, a key-word box and a timeline","explanation":"Biography features.","explanation_ar":"سمات السيرة.","dok":1,"skill":"text features","stage":"freer"},{"q":"List the features in the procedure.","answer":"bold labels (Question, Hypothesis, Materials, Safety), numbered steps, a labelled diagram and section headings","explanation":"Procedure features.","explanation_ar":"سمات الإجراء.","dok":1,"skill":"text features","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Which features are in BOTH texts?","answer":"headings and bold words","explanation":"Both.","explanation_ar":"المشترك.","dok":2,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Which feature helped you most? Why?","answer":"The timeline, because it showed the order of her life in one line and I found the dates quickly.","explanation":"Evaluate.","explanation_ar":"قيّم.","dok":2,"skill":"text features","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Use and create text features.","instructions_ar":"استخدم سمات النص واصنعها.","items":[{"q":"Write a caption for a picture of the paper test.","answer":"A paper test the size of a stamp changes color with one drop of blood.","explanation":"Caption.","explanation_ar":"تعليق.","dok":2,"skill":"creating features","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a key-word box for *hypothesis*.","answer":"Key word: hypothesis – a smart guess that you can test in an experiment.","explanation":"Key-word box.","explanation_ar":"صندوق كلمة.","dok":2,"skill":"creating features","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why did Dr. Sindi want her tests to be cheap and work without electricity?","answer":"So that people who live far from doctors and labs, and who are poor, could still test their health.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"استنتاج.","dok":3,"skill":"inference","stage":"production"},{"q":"How is Dr. Sindi like the engineers in the water-filter text?","answer":"Both use science to solve real problems for people who don't have what they need, like clean water or doctors.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Dr. Hayat Sindi: Science for Everyone\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Dr. Hayat Sindi: Science for Everyone”.","answer":"249 words","title":"Dr. Hayat Sindi: Science for Everyone","body":"**Photo caption:** Dr. Hayat Sindi believes that science should help people everywhere.\n\n**A Dream in Makkah** – Hayat Sindi grew up in Makkah in a large family. As a girl, she read about great scientists such as Ibn al-Haytham and Marie Curie, and she dreamed of becoming a scientist too.\n\n**A Brave Journey** – At first, her family worried about her studying so far from home, but they supported her dream. She traveled to London, worked hard to learn English and then studied science at King's College London. Later, she earned a PhD in **biotechnology** at the University of Cambridge.\n\n**Science for Everyone** – Dr. Sindi wanted her science to help people who live far from doctors and laboratories. With a team of scientists in the United States, she helped start a project that makes tiny **paper tests**, about the size of a postage stamp. With just one drop of blood, the paper changes color to show whether a person may have a health problem. The tests are cheap and do not need electricity.\n\n**Inspiring Others** – In 2012, UNESCO named her a Goodwill Ambassador for science. In 2013, she became one of the first women on Saudi Arabia's Shura Council. She also started an institute that helps young inventors in the Arab world turn their ideas into real products.\n\n**Timeline:** grew up in Makkah → studied in London → PhD at Cambridge → paper tests → UNESCO Goodwill Ambassador (2012) → Shura Council (2013)\n\n**Key word:** *biotechnology* – using living things, such as cells, to make useful products.","words":249,"limit":164}],"time_limit_sec":164},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Text Features Chart","instructions":"Organizer: Write the features you find in each text and what each one helps you do.","items":[{"q":"Features in the biography","answer":"photo caption, headings for life stages, bold word, key-word box, timeline"},{"q":"Features in the procedure","answer":"Question/Hypothesis/Materials/Safety labels, numbered steps 1–6, a labelled diagram, bold section headings"},{"q":"Features in both","answer":"headings and bold words"},{"q":"Which feature helped me most? Why?","answer":"the timeline – it showed the order of her life in one line, so I found dates fast"}]}],"game":{"name":"Feature Finder Race","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Feature finder: a question appears; the child first taps the feature that will answer it fastest, then the answer. Quick, correct choices fill a 'science energy' bar.","The teacher reads a question card.","Teams hold up the feature card they will use.","Then they find the answer in the text.","Correct feature = 1 point; correct answer = 1 point; first team = 1 bonus point.","After ten questions, the highest score wins."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What are *text features*?","answer":"special parts of a text that help readers find and understand information","explanation":"Definition.","explanation_ar":"أجزاء خاصة تساعد القارئ.","options":["special parts of a text that help readers find and understand information","the ordinary sentences in a story","the author's opinions","the page numbers only"],"dok":1,"skill":"text features"},{"q":"What is a *caption*?","answer":"words under a picture that explain it","explanation":"Caption.","explanation_ar":"تعليق على الصورة.","options":["words under a picture that explain it","a kind of heading","a list of steps","a science tool"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*She became a Goodwill **Ambassador** for science.* *Ambassador* means ___.","answer":"a person chosen to represent an idea","explanation":"Ambassador.","explanation_ar":"سفيرة.","options":["a kind of laboratory","a person chosen to represent an idea","a scientist's tool","a student"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which feature shows events in the order they happened?","answer":"a timeline","explanation":"Timeline.","explanation_ar":"الخط الزمني.","options":["a caption","a key-word box","a bold word","a timeline"],"dok":1,"skill":"text features"},{"q":"Which sentence about Dr. Sindi is correct?","answer":"She became one of the first women on the Shura Council.","explanation":"one of the + plural.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["She became one of the first women on the Shura Council.","She became one of the first woman on the Shura Council.","She become one of the first women on the Shura Council.","She became one of first women on the Shura Council."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence about the paper tests is correct?","answer":"The tests don't need electricity.","explanation":"Plural → don't.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The tests doesn't need electricity.","The tests not need electricity.","The tests don't need electricity.","The tests don't needs electricity."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Why did Dr. Sindi's family support her, even though they worried?","answer":"They believed in her dream.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"آمنوا بحلمها.","options":["They wanted her to leave forever.","They believed in her dream.","They didn't care.","They wanted her to be a teacher."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"Which text feature would you ADD to the biography to show how the paper test works?","answer":"a labelled diagram of the paper test","explanation":"Choosing features.","explanation_ar":"رسم موضح.","options":["a safety box about cutting bottles","a labelled diagram of the paper test","a list of materials for a filter","numbered steps for washing hands"],"dok":2,"skill":"text features"},{"q":"What is the main idea of *Dr. Hayat Sindi: Science for Everyone*?","answer":"A Saudi scientist worked hard and used science to help people everywhere.","explanation":"Main idea.","explanation_ar":"عالمة سعودية سخّرت العلم لخدمة الناس.","options":["Cambridge is a university.","Paper is cheap.","A Saudi scientist worked hard and used science to help people everywhere.","Makkah is a big city."],"dok":3,"skill":"main idea"},{"q":"Why do the two texts use DIFFERENT features?","answer":"They have different purposes: one tells how to do an experiment; the other tells about a person's life.","explanation":"Purpose → features.","explanation_ar":"اختلاف الهدف.","options":["One writer was lazy.","Features are random.","Both texts are the same type.","They have different purposes: one tells how to do an experiment; the other tells about a person's life."],"dok":3,"skill":"compare texts"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'biotechnology': a scientist with cells in a dish"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'caption': a photo with words under it"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'timeline': a line with dates and dots"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ambassador': a woman giving a speech with a flag"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'inventor': a young person with a new gadget"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'cell': round cells under a microscope"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'feature': a page with labels pointing to its parts"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-01","prompt":"A stamp-sized paper diagnostic test."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-02","prompt":"An illustrated timeline of a Saudi scientist's life."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS3-03","prompt":"A text features chart with icons."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V01","script":"biotechnology. Dr. Sindi studied biotechnology."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V02","script":"caption. Read the caption under the photo."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V03","script":"timeline. The timeline shows her life in order."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V04","script":"ambassador. She became a Goodwill Ambassador for science."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V05","script":"inventor. The institute helps young inventors."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V06","script":"cell. We saw cells under the microscope."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-V07","script":"feature. Headings are a feature of science texts."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-T1","script":"**Photo caption:** Dr. Hayat Sindi believes that science should help people everywhere. **A Dream in Makkah** – Hayat Sindi grew up in Makkah in a large family. As a girl, she read about great scientists such as Ibn al-Haytham and Marie Curie, and she dreamed of becoming a scientist too. **A Brave Journey** – At first, her family worried about her studying so far from home, but they supported her dream. She traveled to London, worked hard to learn English and then studied science at King's College London. Later, she earned a PhD in **biotechnology** at the University of Cambridge. **Science for Everyone** – Dr. Sindi wanted her science to help people who live far from doctors and laboratories. With a team of scientists in the United States, she helped start a project that makes tiny **paper tests**, about the size of a postage stamp. With just one drop of blood, the paper changes color to show whether a person may have a health problem. The tests are cheap and do not need electricity. **Inspiring Others** – In 2012, UNESCO named her a Goodwill Ambassador for science. In 2013, she became one of the first women on Saudi Arabia's Shura Council. She also started an institute that helps young inventors in the Arab world turn their ideas into real products. **Timeline:** grew up in Makkah → studied in London → PhD at Cambridge → paper tests → UNESCO Goodwill Ambassador (2012) → Shura Council (2013) **Key word:** *biotechnology* – using living things, such as cells, to make useful products."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS3-VID","script":"A girl from Makkah who dreamed of science… and helped people far away from hospitals. Meet Dr. Hayat Sindi! Hayat Sindi grew up in Makkah in a large family… Text features help you find information fast: headings, bold words, captions, timelines, key-word boxes! The procedure uses different features: numbered steps, materials and safety boxes! Which feature answers your question fastest? Scan it!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Explaining a process","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["explain a scientific process in the correct order with sequence words","show how each stage leads to the next with cause–effect words (so, which, as a result, this allows)","define technical terms and use the present simple for processes"],"objectives_ar":["شرح عملية علمية بالترتيب الصحيح بكلمات التسلسل.","إظهار كيف تؤدي كل مرحلة إلى التالية بكلمات السبب والنتيجة.","تعريف المصطلحات العلمية واستخدام المضارع البسيط للعمليات."],"success_criteria":["I can put the stages of a process in order.","I can link stages with sequence and cause–effect words.","I can explain a technical word inside my sentence."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع ترتيب مراحل العملية.","أستطيع ربط المراحل بكلمات التسلسل والسبب والنتيجة.","أستطيع شرح المصطلح العلمي داخل جملتي."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"process","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈprɑsɛs/","definition":"a series of actions or changes that happen in order","arabic":"عملية","example":"Photosynthesis is a process.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"photosynthesis","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌfoʊtoʊˈsɪnθəsɪs/","definition":"the process plants use to make food from sunlight, water and air","arabic":"التمثيل الضوئي","example":"Photosynthesis happens in the leaves.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"chlorophyll","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈklɔrəfɪl/","definition":"the green substance in leaves that captures energy from sunlight","arabic":"اليخضور (الكلوروفيل)","example":"Chlorophyll makes leaves green.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"carbon dioxide","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɑrbən daɪˈɑksaɪd/","definition":"a gas in the air that plants take in and people breathe out","arabic":"ثاني أكسيد الكربون","example":"Leaves take in carbon dioxide.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"capture","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈkæptʃər/","definition":"to catch and keep something","arabic":"يلتقط","example":"Chlorophyll captures energy from sunlight.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"release","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈlis/","definition":"to let something go out","arabic":"يطلق / يُخرج","example":"The leaves release oxygen.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"stage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/steɪdʒ/","definition":"one part of a process","arabic":"مرحلة","example":"There are four stages in this process.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"When you **explain a process**, you tell **how something happens**, step by step. Begin with an **introduction** that names the process and makes the reader curious.\n\nTell the **stages in order** with **sequence words**: *First, Next, Then, At the same time, After that, Finally*. Show **how one stage leads to the next** with **cause–effect words**: *so, which, as a result, this allows, because*.\n\nUse the **present simple** (*the roots take in water*) because processes happen again and again. **Explain technical words** in the sentence (*a green substance called chlorophyll*). End with **why the process matters**.","examples":["Introduction: ***Plants cannot go to a shop, so how do they eat? They make their own food in a process called photosynthesis.***","Stage 1: ***First, the roots take in water from the soil.***","Two things together: ***At the same time, the leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air.***","Defining a term: ***Next, a green substance called chlorophyll captures energy from sunlight.***","Cause → effect: ***The plant uses this energy to make sugar, which is its food. As a result, the plant can grow.***","Last stage: ***Finally, the leaves release oxygen into the air.***","Another process (the water filter): ***First, the gravel traps large dirt, so the water that reaches the sand is already cleaner.***","Another process (the water cycle): ***Water evaporates in the sun; then the vapor cools and forms clouds.***","Conclusion: ***This is why plants are so important: they make the oxygen that we breathe.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Stages in the wrong order. → ✓ Check: does each stage need the one before it? (Logical order.)","✗ Using only 'then… then… then…'. → ✓ First, Next, At the same time, Finally. (Vary the sequence words.)","✗ Not explaining what a technical word means. → ✓ Add: called…, which is…, or a short definition. (Help the reader.)","✗ The roots took in water. → ✓ The roots take in water. (Processes use the **present simple**.)","✗ The leaves releases oxygen. → ✓ The leaves release oxygen. (Plural → **release**.)","✗ Plants make food. Plants grow. → ✓ Plants make food, so they can grow. (Link stages with cause–effect words.)"],"arabic":"عند شرح عملية تخبر القارئ كيف يحدث الشيء خطوة بخطوة، وتبدأ بمقدمة تسمّي العملية وتثير الفضول.\n\nرتّب المراحل بكلمات التسلسل (First, Next, At the same time, Finally)، واربط كل مرحلة بالتي تليها بكلمات السبب والنتيجة (so, which, as a result).\n\nاستخدم المضارع البسيط لأن العمليات تتكرر، واشرح المصطلحات العلمية داخل الجملة، واختم بأهمية العملية.\n\nالتمثيل الضوئي: تأخذ الجذور الماء، وتأخذ الأوراق ثاني أكسيد الكربون، ويلتقط اليخضور طاقة الشمس، فيصنع النبات السكر ويطلق الأكسجين."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Plants can't go shopping… so how do they eat? Adam explains!","on_screen":"Explaining a Process 🌱","visual":"A plant waves at a closed shop; Adam laughs."},{"time":"00:25–01:30","narration":"Plants cannot go to a shop or hunt for dinner…","on_screen":"(Adam's explanation)","visual":"Animation: water rises from roots, carbon dioxide enters leaves, sunlight hits chlorophyll, sugar forms, oxygen bubbles out."},{"time":"01:30–02:20","narration":"Sequence words keep the order: First, Next, At the same time, Finally!","on_screen":"First → Next → Finally","visual":"Numbered arrows connect the stages."},{"time":"02:20–03:00","narration":"Cause and effect show WHY each stage matters: so, which, as a result!","on_screen":"so · which · as a result","visual":"Links glow between the stages."},{"time":"03:00–03:30","narration":"Use the present simple, explain your science words and end with why it matters!","on_screen":"Present simple ✔ Define terms ✔ Why it matters ✔","visual":"A checklist ticks itself."}],"captions_ar":["النباتات لا تذهب للتسوق… فكيف تأكل؟ آدم يشرح!","لا تستطيع النباتات الذهاب إلى متجر أو الصيد…","كلمات التسلسل تحفظ الترتيب: أولًا، بعد ذلك، في الوقت نفسه، أخيرًا!","السبب والنتيجة يوضحان أهمية كل مرحلة: so, which, as a result!","استخدم المضارع البسيط، واشرح كلماتك العلمية، واختم بأهمية العملية!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, clear male science teacher, speed 0.95. Adam reads his explanation."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"How Plants Make Their Own Food (by Adam)","body":"Plants cannot go to a shop or hunt for dinner, so how do they eat? They make their own food in a process called **photosynthesis**.\n\nFirst, the roots take in water from the soil, and the water travels up the stem to the leaves. At the same time, the leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny holes. Next, a green substance in the leaves, called **chlorophyll**, captures energy from sunlight. The plant uses this energy to turn the water and carbon dioxide into sugar, which is its food. As a result, the plant can grow. Finally, the leaves release oxygen into the air. This is why plants are so important: they make food for themselves and the oxygen that we breathe.","word_count":124,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U3-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Introduction:** a question hook + the **name of the process** (*photosynthesis*).","**Sequence words:** *First, At the same time, Next, Finally* – the reader can follow each stage.","**Technical terms explained:** *a green substance in the leaves, called chlorophyll* – a definition clue inside the sentence.","**Cause and effect:** *The plant uses this energy to turn…* / *which is its food* / *As a result, the plant can grow.*","**Present simple tense:** *the roots take in, the water travels, the leaves release* – processes that always happen.","**Conclusion:** *This is why plants are so important…* – why the process matters."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Adam's explanation. Choose the best answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ شرح آدم واختر أفضل إجابة.","items":[{"q":"What is the name of the process Adam explains?","answer":"photosynthesis","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"التمثيل الضوئي.","options":["the water cycle","filtering","breathing","photosynthesis"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What do the roots take in?","answer":"water from the soil","explanation":"First, …","explanation_ar":"الماء.","options":["carbon dioxide","sunlight","sugar","water from the soil"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How does carbon dioxide get into the leaves?","answer":"through tiny holes","explanation":"At the same time, …","explanation_ar":"من خلال ثقوب صغيرة.","options":["through the roots","through tiny holes","through the flowers","through the soil"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What does chlorophyll do?","answer":"captures energy from sunlight","explanation":"Next, …","explanation_ar":"يلتقط طاقة الشمس.","options":["makes the roots grow","captures energy from sunlight","takes in water","releases carbon dioxide"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is the plant's food?","answer":"sugar","explanation":"which is its food.","explanation_ar":"السكر.","options":["sugar","oxygen","soil","chlorophyll"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why does Adam say plants are so important?","answer":"They make food for themselves and the oxygen we breathe.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"يصنعون الغذاء والأكسجين.","options":["They are green.","They live in shops.","They make food for themselves and the oxygen we breathe.","They drink a lot of water."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Explaining a process. Choose.","instructions_ar":"شرح العملية: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which word shows that two stages happen together?","answer":"At the same time","explanation":"Sequence word.","explanation_ar":"في الوقت نفسه.","options":["At the same time","Finally","First","As a result"],"dok":1,"skill":"sequence words","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which phrase in Adam's text shows an EFFECT?","answer":"As a result, the plant can grow.","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"نتيجة.","options":["First, the roots take in water.","Plants cannot go to a shop.","As a result, the plant can grow.","At the same time, the leaves…"],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the leaves uses the correct tense for a process?","answer":"The leaves release oxygen into the air.","explanation":"Present simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The leaves released oxygen into the air yesterday.","The leaves will released oxygen.","The leaves release oxygen into the air.","The leaves releasing oxygen into the air."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How does Adam explain the word *chlorophyll*?","answer":"He says it is a green substance in the leaves.","explanation":"Defining terms.","explanation_ar":"يعرّفه داخل الجملة.","options":["He gives a dictionary page number.","He doesn't explain it.","He draws a picture only.","He says it is a green substance in the leaves."],"dok":2,"skill":"technical terms","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which order of stages for the water filter is correct?","answer":"gravel traps big dirt → sand traps smaller dirt → the coffee filter traps tiny particles","explanation":"Process order.","explanation_ar":"الترتيب الصحيح.","options":["coffee filter → gravel → sand","gravel traps big dirt → sand traps smaller dirt → the coffee filter traps tiny particles","sand → coffee filter → gravel","tiny particles → big dirt → sand"],"dok":2,"skill":"sequence","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence BEST links two stages of the water cycle with cause and effect?","answer":"The sun heats the water, so it evaporates and rises as vapor.","explanation":"Cause → effect.","explanation_ar":"يربط السبب بالنتيجة.","options":["The sun heats the water, so it evaporates and rises as vapor.","The sun is hot. Water is wet.","Water rises. Clouds are white.","The sun heats water and the sea is big."],"dok":3,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the explanation of the water cycle with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل شرح دورة الماء من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"________, the sun heats water in seas and lakes.","answer":"First","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"أولًا.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The water turns into vapor, ________ rises into the sky.","answer":"which","explanation":"Link.","explanation_ar":"الذي.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, the vapor cools down and forms clouds.","answer":"Next","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"بعد ذلك.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The drops in the clouds get heavy, ________ they fall as rain.","answer":"so","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"لذلك.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, rivers fill up and flow back to the sea.","answer":"As a result","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"ونتيجة لذلك.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"________, the cycle begins again.","answer":"Finally","explanation":"Last stage.","explanation_ar":"أخيرًا.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain processes.","instructions_ar":"اشرح العمليات.","items":[{"q":"Explain the first two stages of photosynthesis in your own words.","answer":"First, the roots take in water, which travels up to the leaves. At the same time, the leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air.","explanation":"Two stages.","explanation_ar":"مرحلتان.","dok":2,"skill":"explaining a process","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a sentence that defines a technical term inside it (use *called* or *which is*).","answer":"Water vapor, which is water as an invisible gas, rises into the sky.","explanation":"Define a term.","explanation_ar":"عرّف مصطلحًا.","dok":2,"skill":"technical terms","stage":"production"},{"q":"Explain how the water filter works in three stages with sequence and cause–effect words.","answer":"First, the gravel traps the biggest dirt. Next, the coarse sand catches smaller pieces, so the water gets clearer. Finally, the fine sand and the filter catch the tiniest particles.","explanation":"Process.","explanation_ar":"عملية.","dok":3,"skill":"explaining a process","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a conclusion sentence for the water-filter explanation that tells why it matters.","answer":"This is why filters are important: they help people get cleaner water, although the water still needs treatment before drinking.","explanation":"Why it matters.","explanation_ar":"الأهمية.","dok":3,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Process Chain Reaction","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Chain builder: the child drags stages into order and chooses the best link word between each pair. A correct chain lights up the process animation from start to finish.","Each team gets the stage cards for one process.","They put the stages in order and add a link card between every two stages.","They read the whole process aloud using the links.","Other teams check the order and the links.","Correct order = 3 points; each correct link = 1 point; a defined technical word = 2 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *process*?","answer":"a series of actions or changes that happen in order","explanation":"Process.","explanation_ar":"عملية.","options":["a series of actions or changes that happen in order","a single word","a type of plant","a science tool"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"What do leaves *release* during photosynthesis?","answer":"oxygen","explanation":"Release.","explanation_ar":"الأكسجين.","options":["sugar","oxygen","water","soil"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details"},{"q":"*Chlorophyll **captures** energy from sunlight.* *Captures* means ___.","answer":"catches and keeps","explanation":"Capture.","explanation_ar":"يلتقط.","options":["catches and keeps","throws away","turns off","breaks"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which word BEST starts the last stage of a process?","answer":"Finally,","explanation":"Sequence.","explanation_ar":"أخيرًا.","options":["Finally,","First,","At the same time,","For example,"],"dok":1,"skill":"sequence words"},{"q":"Which sentence about the roots uses the correct tense for a process?","answer":"The roots take in water from the soil.","explanation":"Present simple.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The roots took in water from the soil last year.","The roots will took in water.","The roots taking in water.","The roots take in water from the soil."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense"},{"q":"Which sentence defines a technical term inside it?","answer":"A green substance called chlorophyll captures energy.","explanation":"Define a term.","explanation_ar":"تعرّف المصطلح.","options":["Chlorophyll is in the leaves.","Plants are green.","A green substance called chlorophyll captures energy.","Leaves are nice."],"dok":2,"skill":"technical terms"},{"q":"Which phrase shows a RESULT?","answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"As a result.","options":["At the same time,","First,","Next,","As a result,"],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect"},{"q":"Why should a process explanation end with *why it matters*?","answer":"It shows the reader why the process is important.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"ليوضح أهميته.","options":["It makes the text shorter.","It shows the reader why the process is important.","It repeats the first stage.","It lists the materials."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions"},{"q":"Which stage must come BEFORE the plant can make sugar?","answer":"Chlorophyll captures energy from sunlight.","explanation":"Logical order.","explanation_ar":"التقاط طاقة الشمس.","options":["The leaves release oxygen.","The plant grows taller.","Chlorophyll captures energy from sunlight.","The plant makes flowers."],"dok":3,"skill":"sequence"},{"q":"What would happen to a plant kept in a dark cupboard for weeks? Use the process.","answer":"It could not capture sunlight, so it couldn't make enough food to grow.","explanation":"Apply.","explanation_ar":"لن يصنع غذاءه.","options":["It would grow faster.","It would make more oxygen.","It could not capture sunlight, so it couldn't make enough food to grow.","Nothing would change."],"dok":3,"skill":"apply"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'process': arrows showing steps in a cycle"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'photosynthesis': a leaf with sun, water and air arrows"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'chlorophyll': a close-up of a green leaf"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'carbon dioxide': CO₂ bubbles going into a leaf"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'capture': a net catching light rays"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'release': bubbles leaving a leaf"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'stage': four numbered boxes"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-01","prompt":"A labelled photosynthesis diagram."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-02","prompt":"A process chain with link words."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS4-03","prompt":"Water cycle stage cards."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V01","script":"process. Photosynthesis is a process."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V02","script":"photosynthesis. Photosynthesis happens in the leaves."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V03","script":"chlorophyll. Chlorophyll makes leaves green."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V04","script":"carbon dioxide. Leaves take in carbon dioxide."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V05","script":"capture. Chlorophyll captures energy from sunlight."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V06","script":"release. The leaves release oxygen."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-V07","script":"stage. There are four stages in this process."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-T1","script":"Plants cannot go to a shop or hunt for dinner, so how do they eat? They make their own food in a process called **photosynthesis**. First, the roots take in water from the soil, and the water travels up the stem to the leaves. At the same time, the leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny holes. Next, a green substance in the leaves, called **chlorophyll**, captures energy from sunlight. The plant uses this energy to turn the water and carbon dioxide into sugar, which is its food. As a result, the plant can grow. Finally, the leaves release oxygen into the air. This is why plants are so important: they make food for themselves and the oxygen that we breathe."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS4-VID","script":"Plants can't go shopping… so how do they eat? Adam explains! Plants cannot go to a shop or hunt for dinner… Sequence words keep the order: First, Next, At the same time, Finally! Cause and effect show WHY each stage matters: so, which, as a result! Use the present simple, explain your science words and end with why it matters!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A two-paragraph lab report","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a two-paragraph lab report from lab notes with the Lab Report Planner","write a Method paragraph in the past tense and in order, and a Results paragraph with data and a conclusion (120–150 words)","say whether the results support the hypothesis and suggest one improvement"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لتقرير مخبري من فقرتين انطلاقًا من ملاحظات المختبر.","كتابة فقرة الطريقة بالماضي وبالترتيب، وفقرة النتائج بالبيانات والاستنتاج (120–150 كلمة).","بيان هل تدعم النتائج الفرضية واقتراح تحسين واحد."],"success_criteria":["My Method paragraph tells what we did in order, in the past tense, so someone else could repeat it.","My Results paragraph uses numbers to compare and says if the hypothesis was supported.","I used science words correctly (variable, data, hypothesis, fair test) in 120–150 words."],"success_criteria_ar":["فقرة الطريقة تذكر ما فعلناه بالترتيب وبالماضي بحيث يستطيع غيرنا تكراره.","فقرة النتائج تستخدم الأرقام للمقارنة وتذكر هل دُعمت الفرضية.","استخدمت المصطلحات العلمية بشكل صحيح في 120–150 كلمة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"method","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈmɛθəd/","definition":"the way something is done; the steps of an experiment","arabic":"الطريقة / المنهج","example":"The method explains how we did the experiment.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"fair test","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fɛr tɛst/","definition":"an experiment where only one thing changes and everything else stays the same","arabic":"اختبار عادل","example":"We used the same water to make it a fair test.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"depth","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɛpθ/","definition":"how deep something is","arabic":"العمق","example":"The depth of the sand was 6 cm.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"clarity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈklærəti/","definition":"how clear something is","arabic":"الصفاء / الوضوح","example":"We rated the clarity from 1 to 5.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"scale","pos":"noun","ipa":"/skeɪl/","definition":"a set of numbers used to measure or compare something","arabic":"مقياس / سُلّم","example":"We used a clarity scale of 1 to 5.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"support","pos":"verb","ipa":"/səˈpɔrt/","definition":"(of results) to show that an idea is probably true","arabic":"يدعم / يؤيد","example":"Our results support the hypothesis.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"improvement","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪmˈpruvmənt/","definition":"a change that makes something better","arabic":"تحسين","example":"One improvement would be to test each filter three times.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **lab report** tells other scientists **what you did** and **what you found**, so they can check your work or repeat it.\n\n**Paragraph 1 – Method:** state the **question** (or aim), describe the **set-up** (what was the same, what was different) and tell the **steps in order** in the **past tense**: *First, we built…; Then we poured…; We timed…*. Name the **variable** and explain why it was a **fair test**.\n\n**Paragraph 2 – Results:** give the **data** with **numbers** and **compare** them (*clearer, slower, less*); say whether the results **support** the hypothesis; add **one improvement** for next time. Keep it **120–150 words**, **objective** (no 'I loved it!') and accurate.","examples":["Aim: ***We investigated whether a deeper layer of fine sand makes muddy water clearer.***","Set-up: ***We built two filters. Filter A had 3 cm of fine sand, and Filter B had 6 cm. Everything else was the same.***","Steps: ***First, we poured 200 mL of the same muddy water into each filter. Then we timed how long it took and measured the water collected.***","Fair test: ***Only the depth of the sand changed, so it was a fair test.***","Results with numbers: ***Filter B's water was clearer (4 out of 5) than Filter A's (3 out of 5).***","Comparing: ***However, Filter B was slower (3 min 40 s compared with 2 min 10 s) and collected 10 mL less water.***","Conclusion: ***These results support our hypothesis that a deeper sand layer makes water clearer.***","Improvement: ***Next time, we would test each filter three times to make our results more reliable.***","Not objective: *It was the best experiment ever!* → keep feelings out of a lab report."],"common_errors":["✗ Writing the method as instructions (Pour the water…). → ✓ Report what you did: We poured the water… (Past tense, not commands.)","✗ Results with no numbers. → ✓ Use the data: 4 out of 5, 3 min 40 s. (Numbers are evidence.)","✗ Our hypothesis was right 100%. → ✓ Our results support the hypothesis. (Use careful science language.)","✗ We measured 200 ml of water. → ✓ We measured 200 mL of water. (Unit symbol: **mL**.)","✗ Filter B was more clear than A. → ✓ Filter B was clearer than Filter A. (Short adjective → **-er**.)","✗ Filter B was more slow. → ✓ Filter B was slower. (slow → **slower**.)","✗ I loved this experiment so much! → ✓ (Leave feelings out.) (Lab reports are objective.)"],"arabic":"التقرير المخبري يخبر العلماء الآخرين بما فعلت وما وجدت، ليتحققوا من عملك أو يكرروه.\n\nالفقرة الأولى (الطريقة): السؤال، والإعداد (ما الثابت وما المتغيّر)، والخطوات بالترتيب وبالماضي، وسبب كونه اختبارًا عادلًا.\n\nالفقرة الثانية (النتائج): البيانات بالأرقام والمقارنة، وهل تدعم النتائج الفرضية، وتحسين واحد للمرة القادمة.\n\nاكتب بموضوعية ودقة دون مشاعر شخصية، والتزم بطول 120–150 كلمة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Scientists don't just do experiments – they report them! Let's write like real scientists.","on_screen":"My Lab Report 🧪📝","visual":"Noura opens a lab notebook full of numbers."},{"time":"00:25–01:10","narration":"Two filters: 3 cm of sand and 6 cm of sand. Same water, same bottle – only one variable!","on_screen":"Fair test ⚖️","visual":"Two filters side by side with a ruler on the sand."},{"time":"01:10–02:10","narration":"We investigated whether a deeper layer of fine sand makes muddy water clearer…","on_screen":"(model report)","visual":"Timers, measuring cups and a 1–5 clarity scale appear as the report is read."},{"time":"02:10–03:00","narration":"Paragraph 1: Method – past tense and in order. Paragraph 2: Results – numbers, comparison, conclusion, improvement!","on_screen":"Method · Results","visual":"Two blocks build with labels."},{"time":"03:00–03:30","narration":"Be objective, be accurate – and never drink your filtered water!","on_screen":"Report like a scientist 🔬","visual":"A gold 'Junior Scientist' badge appears."}],"captions_ar":["العلماء لا يجرون التجارب فقط، بل يكتبون تقارير عنها! لنكتب كالعلماء.","مرشحان: 3 سم من الرمل و6 سم. الماء نفسه والقارورة نفسها؛ متغيّر واحد فقط!","استقصينا هل تجعل طبقةُ رمل ناعم أعمق الماءَ الموحل أصفى…","الفقرة الأولى: الطريقة بالماضي وبالترتيب. الفقرة الثانية: النتائج بالأرقام والمقارنة والاستنتاج والتحسين!","كن موضوعيًا ودقيقًا، ولا تشرب الماء المرشّح أبدًا!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, precise female scientist voice, speed 0.95. Noura and Adam read their report."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Lab Notebook: Does a Deeper Sand Layer Make Clearer Water?","body":"**Question:** Does a deeper layer of fine sand make muddy water clearer? · **Hypothesis:** If the sand layer is deeper, then the water will be clearer, because more sand can trap more dirt.\n\n**Set-up:** Filter A – fine sand 3 cm, coarse sand 3 cm, gravel 3 cm · Filter B – fine sand 6 cm, coarse sand 3 cm, gravel 3 cm · Same bottle size, same cotton ball and coffee filter · Same muddy water (stirred), 200 mL for each filter\n\n**Clarity scale:** 1 = very muddy, 5 = very clear · Muddy water before filtering = 1\n\n**Data – Filter A:** time 2 min 10 s · water collected 170 mL · clarity 3\n\n**Data – Filter B:** time 3 min 40 s · water collected 160 mL · clarity 4\n\n**Notes:** Filter B dripped more slowly. Both cups still looked a little yellow. Nobody drank the water!","word_count":135,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U3-LS5-1","genre":"lab notebook","role":"model","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[],"annotations":["The **variable** we changed: the depth of the fine sand (3 cm vs. 6 cm).","Everything else stayed the **same** – this makes it a **fair test**.","**Data** are numbers you can compare: time, amount and clarity.","In a lab report, write the **method** in the **past tense** (*we built, we poured, we timed*) and the **results** with the **numbers**."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the lab notebook. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ دفتر المختبر واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What was the VARIABLE in this experiment?","answer":"the depth of the fine sand","explanation":"Set-up.","explanation_ar":"عمق الرمل الناعم.","options":["the size of the bottle","the kind of muddy water","the amount of gravel","the depth of the fine sand"],"dok":1,"skill":"variables","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How much fine sand did Filter B have?","answer":"6 cm","explanation":"Set-up.","explanation_ar":"6 سم.","options":["3 cm","9 cm","6 cm","200 mL"],"dok":1,"skill":"data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How long did Filter A take?","answer":"2 min 10 s","explanation":"Data.","explanation_ar":"دقيقتان و10 ثوانٍ.","options":["3 min 40 s","2 min 10 s","1 minute","5 minutes"],"dok":1,"skill":"data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which filter collected MORE water?","answer":"Filter A (170 mL)","explanation":"Data.","explanation_ar":"المرشح A.","options":["Filter B (160 mL)","Filter A (170 mL)","They collected the same.","Neither collected water."],"dok":1,"skill":"data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did 5 mean on the clarity scale?","answer":"very clear","explanation":"Scale.","explanation_ar":"صافٍ جدًا.","options":["very clear","very muddy","very fast","very slow"],"dok":1,"skill":"data","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did the notebook say *Nobody drank the water!*?","answer":"A simple filter cannot remove germs, so the water is not safe to drink.","explanation":"Safety (Lesson 2).","explanation_ar":"لأن المرشح لا يزيل الجراثيم.","options":["The water tasted bad.","The cups were too small.","The teacher was thirsty.","A simple filter cannot remove germs, so the water is not safe to drink."],"dok":2,"skill":"safety","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Writing the report. Choose.","instructions_ar":"كتابة التقرير: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which sentence is written correctly for the METHOD of the filter report?","answer":"We poured 200 mL of muddy water into each filter.","explanation":"Past tense report.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We poured 200 mL of muddy water into each filter.","Pour 200 mL of muddy water into each filter.","We pour 200 mL of muddy water yesterday.","We will pour 200 mL into each filter."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why was the filter experiment a FAIR test?","answer":"Only the depth of the sand changed; everything else stayed the same.","explanation":"Fair test.","explanation_ar":"متغيّر واحد فقط.","options":["Both filters were in the same room.","Only the depth of the sand changed; everything else stayed the same.","The students worked hard.","The water was muddy."],"dok":2,"skill":"fair test","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence compares the clarity results correctly?","answer":"Filter B's water was clearer than Filter A's.","explanation":"Comparative.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Filter B's water was clearer than Filter A's.","Filter B's water was more clear than Filter A's.","Filter B's water was clearest than Filter A's.","Filter B's water clearer than Filter A's."],"dok":2,"skill":"comparatives","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which conclusion matches the data?","answer":"The results support the hypothesis that a deeper sand layer makes water clearer.","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"استنتاج يطابق البيانات.","options":["The results show that sand makes water dirtier.","The results prove the water is safe to drink.","The results support the hypothesis that a deeper sand layer makes water clearer.","The results show that time does not matter."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence is NOT suitable for a lab report?","answer":"This was the most amazing experiment in the world!","explanation":"Objective tone.","explanation_ar":"غير مناسب.","options":["Filter B was slower than Filter A.","We rated the clarity from 1 to 5.","Next time, we would repeat the test three times.","This was the most amazing experiment in the world!"],"dok":2,"skill":"objective tone","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which improvement would make the results MORE reliable?","answer":"Test each filter three times and compare the results.","explanation":"Reliability.","explanation_ar":"تكرار الاختبار.","options":["Use a different muddy water for each filter.","Change the sand and the gravel at the same time.","Test each filter three times and compare the results.","Only test Filter A."],"dok":3,"skill":"improvements","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your report with the Lab Report Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لتقريرك بمخطط التقرير المخبري.","items":[{"q":"Aim and set-up – write notes.","answer":"Aim: does deeper fine sand make water clearer? Set-up: two filters; A = 3 cm, B = 6 cm; everything else the same.","explanation":"Aim + set-up.","explanation_ar":"الهدف + الإعداد.","dok":1,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Method steps – write notes in the past tense.","answer":"poured 200 mL into each filter; timed it; measured the water collected; rated the clarity 1–5","explanation":"Steps.","explanation_ar":"الخطوات.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Results – write the data you will use.","answer":"A: 2 min 10 s, 170 mL, clarity 3; B: 3 min 40 s, 160 mL, clarity 4","explanation":"Data.","explanation_ar":"البيانات.","dok":1,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Conclusion and improvement – write notes.","answer":"B clearer but slower → supports the hypothesis; next time test each filter three times","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"الاستنتاج.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and publish your lab report.","instructions_ar":"اكتب تقريرك وحسّنه ودققه وانشره.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1: the Method.","answer":"We investigated whether a deeper layer of fine sand makes muddy water clearer. We built two filters: Filter A had 3 cm of fine sand, and Filter B had 6 cm. Everything else was the same. First, we poured 200 mL of muddy water into each. Then we timed the filtering and rated the clarity.","explanation":"Method.","explanation_ar":"الطريقة.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2: the Results with numbers.","answer":"Filter B's water was clearer (4 out of 5) than Filter A's (3 out of 5). However, it was slower: 3 min 40 s compared with 2 min 10 s.","explanation":"Results.","explanation_ar":"النتائج.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write your conclusion and one improvement.","answer":"These results support our hypothesis. Next time, we would test each filter three times to make the results more reliable.","explanation":"Conclusion + improvement.","explanation_ar":"استنتاج + تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence you made more scientific after the Two-Partner Edit (before → after).","answer":"Before: Filter B was way better! After: Filter B's water was clearer (4 out of 5) than Filter A's (3 out of 5).","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Lab Report Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Use the lab notebook. Write short notes for each part.","items":[{"q":"Aim / question","answer":"Does a deeper layer of fine sand make muddy water clearer?"},{"q":"Method – set-up (same / different)","answer":"two filters; A = 3 cm fine sand, B = 6 cm; same bottle, filters and muddy water"},{"q":"Method – steps (past tense)","answer":"poured 200 mL into each; timed; measured water collected; rated clarity 1–5"},{"q":"Results – data (numbers)","answer":"A: 2 min 10 s, 170 mL, clarity 3 · B: 3 min 40 s, 160 mL, clarity 4"},{"q":"Results – conclusion (hypothesis supported?)","answer":"B clearer but slower → supports the hypothesis"},{"q":"Improvement for next time","answer":"test each filter three times; use a clarity chart with photos"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U3-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Use the lab notebook. Write a two-paragraph lab report (120–150 words). Paragraph 1 – Method: the aim, the set-up (what was the same and what was different) and the steps in the past tense. Paragraph 2 – Results: the data with numbers, a comparison, whether the results support the hypothesis, and one improvement. Use the lab notebook. Write a two-paragraph lab report (120–150 words). Paragraph 1 – Method: the aim, the set-up (what was the same and what was different) and the steps in the past tense. Paragraph 2 – Results: the data with numbers, a comparison, whether the results support the hypothesis, and one improvement.","instructions_ar":"استخدم دفتر المختبر واكتب تقريرًا مخبريًا من فقرتين (120–150 كلمة). الفقرة الأولى (الطريقة): الهدف والإعداد (الثابت والمتغيّر) والخطوات بالماضي. الفقرة الثانية (النتائج): البيانات بالأرقام والمقارنة وهل تدعم النتائج الفرضية وتحسين واحد.","prompt":"Use the lab notebook. Write a two-paragraph lab report (120–150 words). Paragraph 1 – Method: the aim, the set-up (what was the same and what was different) and the steps in the past tense. Paragraph 2 – Results: the data with numbers, a comparison, whether the results support the hypothesis, and one improvement.","checklist":["I stated the aim or question.","I described the set-up: what was the same and what was different (the variable).","I wrote the method in order and in the past tense.","I explained why it was a fair test.","I used at least three numbers from the data.","I compared the results (clearer, slower, less).","I said whether the results support the hypothesis and gave one improvement.","My report is objective, has two paragraphs and 120–150 words, and uses units correctly (mL, cm, s)."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":120,"max_words":170,"organizer":"Lab Report Planner (aim – set-up – steps – data – conclusion – improvement)","items":[{"q":"Use the lab notebook. Write a two-paragraph lab report (120–150 words). Paragraph 1 – Method: the aim, the set-up (what was the same and what was different) and the steps in the past tense. Paragraph 2 – Results: the data with numbers, a comparison, whether the results support the hypothesis, and one improvement.","answer":"We investigated whether a deeper layer of fine sand makes muddy water clearer. We built two filters in the same kind of bottle. Filter A had 3 cm of fine sand, and Filter B had 6 cm. Everything else was the same, so it was a fair test. First, we poured 200 mL of the same muddy water into each filter. Then we timed the filtering, measured the water collected and rated its clarity from 1 to 5. (new paragraph) Filter B's water was clearer (4) than Filter A's (3). However, Filter B was slower (3 min 40 s compared with 2 min 10 s) and collected 10 mL less water. These results support our hypothesis that a deeper sand layer makes water clearer. Next time, we would test each filter three times to make our results more reliable.","_w":{"checklist":["I stated the aim or question.","I described the set-up: what was the same and what was different (the variable).","I wrote the method in order and in the past tense.","I explained why it was a fair test.","I used at least three numbers from the data.","I compared the results (clearer, slower, less).","I said whether the results support the hypothesis and gave one improvement.","My report is objective, has two paragraphs and 120–150 words, and uses units correctly (mL, cm, s)."],"min":120,"max":170}}]}],"game":{"name":"Junior Scientist Report Check","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Science editor: sentences scroll in; the child sorts each into Method or Results, then marks it ✓ or ✗. For ✗ sentences, the child chooses the fixed version. Correct edits earn a 'Junior Scientist' badge.","Teams get a pile of sentence cards.","They sort each into METHOD or RESULTS.","They mark weak sentences (commands, no numbers, feelings, wrong tense) and rewrite one.","Teams compare with the answer key.","Correct tray = 1 point; correct fix = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What does the METHOD part of a lab report tell?","answer":"what you did, in order","explanation":"Method.","explanation_ar":"ما فعلته بالترتيب.","options":["how you felt","what you did, in order","the title only","a list of jokes"],"dok":1,"skill":"lab reports"},{"q":"What is a *fair test*?","answer":"an experiment where only one thing changes","explanation":"Fair test.","explanation_ar":"متغيّر واحد فقط.","options":["a test with no questions","an experiment where only one thing changes","an experiment with many variables","a test you cannot repeat"],"dok":1,"skill":"fair test"},{"q":"*We rated the **clarity** of the water.* *Clarity* means ___.","answer":"how clear something is","explanation":"Clarity.","explanation_ar":"الصفاء.","options":["how hot something is","how heavy something is","how fast something is","how clear something is"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which tense should you use in the Method?","answer":"the past tense","explanation":"Tense.","explanation_ar":"الماضي.","options":["the future tense","commands (imperatives)","the present continuous","the past tense"],"dok":1,"skill":"verb tense"},{"q":"Which sentence about Filter B's speed is correct?","answer":"Filter B was slower than Filter A.","explanation":"Comparative.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Filter B was slower than Filter A.","Filter B was more slow than Filter A.","Filter B was slowest than Filter A.","Filter B slower than Filter A."],"dok":2,"skill":"comparatives"},{"q":"Which unit is written correctly?","answer":"200 mL","explanation":"Unit symbol.","explanation_ar":"200 mL.","options":["200 Ml","200 mL","200 ML.","200 ml s"],"dok":2,"skill":"units"},{"q":"What should the Results paragraph include?","answer":"data with numbers, a comparison and a conclusion","explanation":"Results.","explanation_ar":"البيانات والمقارنة والاستنتاج.","options":["only the materials","only your feelings","the safety rules","data with numbers, a comparison and a conclusion"],"dok":2,"skill":"lab reports"},{"q":"Why should a lab report be OBJECTIVE?","answer":"so other scientists can trust and check the results","explanation":"Objective tone.","explanation_ar":"ليثق العلماء بالنتائج.","options":["so other scientists can trust and check the results","so it is more exciting","so it is shorter","so nobody reads it"],"dok":2,"skill":"objective tone"},{"q":"Filter B had MORE sand. Why do you think it was SLOWER?","answer":"More sand made it harder for the water to pass through.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"الرمل الأكثر أبطأ مرور الماء.","options":["The water in Filter B was hotter.","Filter B was a different color.","More sand made it harder for the water to pass through.","Filter B had no gravel."],"dok":3,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"A student writes: *Our results prove that sand cleans all germs.* What is WRONG?","answer":"The experiment only measured clarity; it cannot show anything about germs.","explanation":"Evaluate claims.","explanation_ar":"التجربة لم تقس الجراثيم.","options":["Nothing is wrong.","It uses the past tense.","The experiment only measured clarity; it cannot show anything about germs.","It is too short."],"dok":3,"skill":"evaluating conclusions"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'method': a numbered list in a lab notebook"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fair test': two identical set-ups with one difference"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'depth': a ruler beside a layer of sand"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'clarity': five cups from muddy to clear"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'scale': a number line from 1 to 5"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'support': a check mark next to a hypothesis"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'improvement': an arrow going up"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-01","prompt":"A student lab report with a bar chart."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-02","prompt":"A lab notebook page with data."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U3-LS5-03","prompt":"A clipboard-shaped lab report planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V01","script":"method. The method explains how we did the experiment."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V02","script":"fair test. We used the same water to make it a fair test."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V03","script":"depth. The depth of the sand was 6 cm."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V04","script":"clarity. We rated the clarity from 1 to 5."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V05","script":"scale. We used a clarity scale of 1 to 5."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V06","script":"support. Our results support the hypothesis."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-V07","script":"improvement. One improvement would be to test each filter three times."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-T1","script":"**Question:** Does a deeper layer of fine sand make muddy water clearer? · **Hypothesis:** If the sand layer is deeper, then the water will be clearer, because more sand can trap more dirt. **Set-up:** Filter A – fine sand 3 cm, coarse sand 3 cm, gravel 3 cm · Filter B – fine sand 6 cm, coarse sand 3 cm, gravel 3 cm · Same bottle size, same cotton ball and coffee filter · Same muddy water (stirred), 200 mL for each filter **Clarity scale:** 1 = very muddy, 5 = very clear · Muddy water before filtering = 1 **Data – Filter A:** time 2 min 10 s · water collected 170 mL · clarity 3 **Data – Filter B:** time 3 min 40 s · water collected 160 mL · clarity 4 **Notes:** Filter B dripped more slowly. Both cups still looked a little yellow. Nobody drank the water!"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U3-LS5-VID","script":"Scientists don't just do experiments – they report them! Let's write like real scientists. Two filters: 3 cm of sand and 6 cm of sand. Same water, same bottle – only one variable! We investigated whether a deeper layer of fine sand makes muddy water clearer… Paragraph 1: Method – past tense and in order. Paragraph 2: Results – numbers, comparison, conclusion, improvement! Be objective, be accurate – and never drink your filtered water!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Before the experiment, Adam wrote: *If plants get too much water, then they will droop.* This is a ___.","answer":"hypothesis","explanation":"Hypothesis.","explanation_ar":"فرضية.","options":["hypothesis","conclusion","laboratory","microscope"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The heights and notes Layla wrote every evening were her ___.","answer":"data","explanation":"Data.","explanation_ar":"بياناتها.","options":["procedure","data","variable","telescope"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Use a ruler to ___ how tall the plant is.","answer":"measure","explanation":"Measure.","explanation_ar":"تقيس.","options":["predict","invest","release","measure"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"After studying the results, the class wrote its ___: plants need the right amount of water.","answer":"conclusion","explanation":"Conclusion.","explanation_ar":"استنتاجه.","options":["conclusion","hypothesis","materials","caption"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"A tool for looking at stars and planets far away is a ___.","answer":"telescope","explanation":"tele = far.","explanation_ar":"مقراب (تلسكوب).","options":["microscope","thermometer","telescope","photograph"],"dok":1,"skill":"Greek roots","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*A **micrometer** is a unit for measuring very small things.* Using the roots, *micrometer* means ___.","answer":"a measure for very small things","explanation":"micro + meter.","explanation_ar":"مقياس للأشياء الصغيرة.","options":["a tool for far-away things","a picture made with light","a story of a life","a measure for very small things"],"dok":2,"skill":"Greek roots","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Measure carefully so your results are **accurate**.* *Accurate* means ___.","answer":"exactly correct","explanation":"Accurate.","explanation_ar":"دقيقة.","options":["very fast","exactly correct","very colorful","not finished"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Scientists **analyze** data to find patterns.* *Analyze* means ___.","answer":"study carefully to understand","explanation":"Analyze.","explanation_ar":"يحللون.","options":["throw away","guess quickly","study carefully to understand","draw a picture"],"dok":2,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence for the METHOD of Layla's report is correct?","answer":"We gave Plant A a big glass of water every day.","explanation":"Past tense report.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Give Plant A a big glass of water every day.","We gave Plant A a big glass of water every day.","We give Plant A a big glass of water last week.","We will gave Plant A water."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence compares the basil plants correctly?","answer":"Plant B grew taller than Plant A.","explanation":"Comparative.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Plant B grew taller than Plant A.","Plant B grew more tall than Plant A.","Plant B grew tallest than Plant A.","Plant B growed taller than Plant A."],"dok":2,"skill":"comparatives","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which conclusion about the basil is written in careful science language?","answer":"These results support the hypothesis that too much water can harm basil.","explanation":"Careful language.","explanation_ar":"لغة علمية حذرة.","options":["These results prove that water is always bad.","Basil is the worst plant ever.","We are 100% sure about all plants.","These results support the hypothesis that too much water can harm basil."],"dok":2,"skill":"conclusions","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence explains a step of a process with cause and effect?","answer":"Too much water fills the spaces in the soil, so the roots cannot get air.","explanation":"Cause → effect.","explanation_ar":"سبب ← نتيجة.","options":["The roots are under the soil.","Basil is green.","Too much water fills the spaces in the soil, so the roots cannot get air.","Layla likes basil."],"dok":3,"skill":"explaining a process","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about bacteria defines a technical term inside it?","answer":"Bacteria, which are tiny living things, can only be seen with a microscope.","explanation":"Define a term.","explanation_ar":"تعريف داخل الجملة.","options":["Bacteria, which are tiny living things, can only be seen with a microscope.","Bacteria are bad.","I saw bacteria.","Bacteria and cells."],"dok":2,"skill":"technical terms","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which improvement would make Layla's results MORE reliable?","answer":"Test several plants in each group, not just one.","explanation":"Reliability.","explanation_ar":"اختبار نباتات أكثر.","options":["Water both plants the same.","Use a different windowsill each day.","Test several plants in each group, not just one.","Stop measuring the plants."],"dok":3,"skill":"improvements","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Use the information in The Mystery of the Drooping Basil. Write a two-paragraph lab report (120–150 words) as if you were Layla. Paragraph 1 – Method: the question, the hypothesis, the set-up (what was the same and what was different) and the steps in the past tense. Paragraph 2 – Results: the data, a comparison, whether the results support the hypothesis, and one improvement.","model":"We investigated why my basil plant was drooping. Our hypothesis was that it was getting too much water. I used two basil plants of the same size and put them on the same sunny windowsill, so only the amount of water changed. For two weeks, I gave Plant A a big glass of water every day and Plant B a small cup only when the soil felt dry. Every evening, I measured their height and recorded my observations. (new paragraph) After two weeks, Plant B had grown 3 cm and had shiny green leaves. Plant A had grown less than 1 cm, its leaves were yellow, and its soil smelled bad. These results support our hypothesis that too much water can harm basil. Next time, I would test five plants in each group to make the results more reliable.","marks":20}});
